      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>mcDOUGALL'S Pateqt SELF-RAISING FLOUR AMTVIEDD. The Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905</p>
        <p>Then be went upstairs to</p>
        <p>time he got round. bath and change. Alter a consultation with the two inspec- tors Verschoyle, ace: ,inpanid by them, drove round to the Kum: S.N.:pitary's house. Though only a luarter to eight, the house showed signs of unusual animation, and he was taken straight vtp, to Mr. Colgate's private sitting-room—tht'roOm in which he had told him of Sir Henry Mornington's inurcler.</p>
        <p>She started as she read it, and :lera handed it to the duke. " Great Heavens." she exclaimed, " what do you think she has done ? Actually written to the Kinghimselfto exldain the cause of her absence He has replied most cordially, and I suppose we shall have to pretend to recog- nise her engagement to Lord Ben Nevis WI— WI Sunday." The duke muttered something which sounded suspiciously like an oath. "She and that fortune-hunting pauper will spell every- thing between them,' he ejaculated angrily. " Everything would have been all right if that beetle-browed fool of a Branksea hadn't bungled his part of the business I " "Unless Ben Nevis dies," began the duchess and then she stopped, not daring to face the future without that event, yet hardly brutal enough to put her wish into words. . Her husband turned sharply' on his heel ' and left the room, not trusting himself to speak. However, by the time that the duke and duchess met their guests at an informal break- fast their equanimity was outwardly. at rate, quite restored. The talk was general and turned naturally upon the arrival of the royal party, and of those in the conspiracy and those outside it, . the former seemed the more enthusiastic at the prospect. "Please all amuse yourselves this morning." said the duke as he rose from the table. "I have to see a whole host of people about the final arrangements, and I'm afraid the duchess will be busy, too. But Charlie can show those of you who wish some fair sport amongst the snipe and a few woodcock, too. It's a pity we're just too late for the pheasants. By the way, Branksea," he concluded, "can you spare me a few minutes ? " The younger man assented willingly and followed him out of the room, limping rather painfully. "What a sham Lord Branksea has strained himself so badly," remarked the duchess pointedly to his mother ; "it seems to hurt him to walk. Still, it won't prevent him from motoring; and he, can, take the King out on the new car the duke's so keen on just I now."</p>
        <p>t.,at out ot bag; be warneo.—t3tnn.- The duke groaned, and the sweat started out upon his brow in beads.</p>
        <p>" It's—it's--" he began. "The worst 1" said his wife firmly,. com- pleting his sentence ; " and that means for most of us death. Anything is preferable LO dishonour." ,</p>
        <p>(To be costinued.)</p>
        <p>The Home Secretary was - filly dressed, except for a smoking jacket, and was busily ;canning the papers. • He jumped no anxiously as Sir Hugo enteted, and •hurried fofward to meet him. "Not another of th:.se murders, I trust?" he asked in an agitated voice. No. thank God," answered the other. "On the other band, the mystery is solved!" "Solved?" exclaimed the Home Secretary incredulouAy. . "I can .hardly believe you. Have you got the murderer under lock and key?" . . "Notget," replied Verschoyle, smiling ai the oilier doubtful face; " and that's what I've come to consult j.ou about."</p>
        <p>Taking a Feat, li told his strange story to the gapiag Minister in as few words as possi- ble, impcded only ty co:istant interjections from the listener.</p>
        <p>"Impossible," declared Mr. Colgate de- cisively. "ac.liiely impoAsible I It must be some phantom of poor Ben Nevis's brain, distorted by the awful shock."</p>
        <p>Versehoyle set his lips grimly. He had half expected such a reception to his story, but it did not ark:et his determination. "Whether you believe it or not, Mr. Col: gate," he said coolly and calmly, "neverthe- less you will be good enough to go down to Windsor by the 9.15 and interview his Majesty personally. On no account must the King go to Soutca. Castle to-day." The Home Secretary intervened abruptly. " A political plot in the twentieth century is imix.i,ssible, uttarly impossible. The whole thing's absurd."</p>
        <p>"So I should have said myself," rejoined Verschoyle drily, "had not facts and my wits convinced roe to the contrary." . .</p>
        <p>" I can't believe it," went on the Cabinet Minister, as he paced the room ill at ease. "The Duke of Hampstead is above suspicion." " So was Ca•sar's wife till she got mixed up in a scandal," said Sir Hugo sharply. " You can't argue with facts. my dear sir ; you can only 'draw it ctiiis fro:n them. Read these papers I have prepared for the King's perusal, . and try to realise the responsibility which will rest upon you, if there is any truth in them."</p>
        <p>It the study Branksea sat down with a groan. "It's that damned dog," he said savagely in explanation. "The brute got his teeth right into my calf, and it's looking very Angry to-day. He spoilt the whole thing, but," he</p>
        <p>He handed the Home Secretary a long open envelope, and ia his turn paced the room, while the other read. For several minutes there was silence. Colgate read with knitted brows, as though puzzled by the lucid statement before him. At last he spoke as he refolded the papers. . . _</p>
        <p>"Good God, it's too awful 1 • I can hardly realise it."</p>
        <p>"Look at the party to meet the King at Southsea Castle," went on Verschoyle, taking no notice of his • ejaculation, "half of them in the conspiracy. There is no saying what might not happen to his Majesty in such com- pany." "Yes, something must be done," said Col- gate, who appeared stunned at the news and the magnitude of the crisis.</p>
        <p>"Yes, you must go to Windsor by the 9.15, as I said before, and place the whole matter .before the. King personally. He goes straight from Windsor to South- sea Castle, changing from the Great Western on to the London and South Western at Basingstoke, so the only way to catch him is to go down .in person. . I've got I3elleville and Blew, the two smartest men at the Yard. here all ready to accompany you, and they will not leave the King--until further orders. His life may be in danger for all we know. He must keep strictly to his private apartments until we have these conspirators laid by the heels. Mind, please, suggest strongly to his Majesty to do nothing personally in the matter —merely to wire the Duke of Hampstead some time about noon that he is laid up with a slight attack of influenza. By the way, you had better wire the King's secretary at once that you want an audience at eleven upon urgent business." The Home Secrotary nodded. "And you ? " he asked, having recovered his composure. "What are you going to do ? "</p>
        <p>I am going to stay in town ,and lay my plans for the arrests, which will be, a matter of no small difficulty; but I shall take no 07cisive step, beyond • preparing the writs, until you return from Windsor and acquaint me with his Majesty's wishes. I have sent a good man down to watch the party at Soutlisea Castle, and he will wire me how the news of the postponement of the King's visit is re- ceived. I expect .the party will break up at once and adjourn to town. If so, I should not be surprised if they, hold an emergency meet- ing at Hampstead rouse Jo-night and that would be the best time to take the lot. Mind, we have not sufficient proof against the majority of them, only strong ground for suspicion, so it would he hettnr to take them red-handed.: HoWever, I will meet you :at the Home Office at three o'clock, and we can lay our final plans then; I;have plenty to do in the meantime." The Home Secretary' nodded again. . . .</p>
        <p>„ ” That will be best. I'll write my tele- gram to Windsor at, once. I wonder how the King will take the news ? It will be an awful shock to him. I'm afraid, but I'm quite with you that on no account must he go to South- sea Castle, whatever happens. By God, poor Mornington was right \ vin he said it would be the greatest sensation of the century " " We have just time for breakfast before you start," said Verschoyle, consulting ',his watch.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXII.</p>
        <p>TIIE great day dawned brightly and auspiciimq at Southsea Castle. The air was keen and frosty, but the wintry sun shone un- usually briglit- and gay. The whole neighbourhood Was agog at the prospect of the Ning's visit, and great plans had been laid for his reception. The drive from the station was decorated with several triumphal arches, . and all the men on the estate had arranged to line the route with torches and to torn the early cvenilig into a scene of blazing light. Nothing had been spared to Make his Majesty's reeeption gorgeous and imposing either at the Castle or in its vicinity. . .</p>
        <p>The Duke of Hampstead was up early and wandering about the place in a state of sup- pressed excitement in the iealisation of the crowning sensation which was at hapd. In his condition of mind Bina had hardly entered his thoughts after his first burst of irritation upon hearing of her decided refusal. Otto, of course, he had left in town under the 'tutelage of lan Frazer, who was not persona grata at court. His nanfe had at first been tentatively included in the list, but had been struck out, when submitted to the Rini,''. Of the other conspirators Lord Isling- ton,Mr. and Mrs. Knox, Sir Napier Cording- ley, Sir Andrew Wentworth, 31r. Brinckley, Mr. Godsmark, and Reginald Carruthers were not of the party, as the Duchess of Hamp- stead had realised that it was useless to suggest their names._</p>
        <p>'Soon after the duke's. appearance down- stairs the past - bag artived, And he eagerly opened and examined its contents. There was little of importance in it, except the royal approval of the final arrangements. He noticed Dina's letter to her mother, and, hoping that it m'g'tt portend submission, he took it up persont.lly to the duchess' dressing- rOOttl.</p>
        <p>The Duchess ,of Hampstead was in the throes of an unusually 'early toilet, but this to her was not so sti.ous a matter as to sonic of her sisters in society. as she still relied more upon nature than upon art for the ultimate effect.</p>
        <p>She Was in a morning wrap and, at a sign from her husband, dismissed her maid.</p>
        <p>Having read her the letter from the King's secretary, he nanded her l3ina's. "A letter from Bina,l he said, simulating Carelessness. " What's in it ? "</p>
        <p>The duchess tore it open eagerly. "Poor child," she said sympathetically, as she unfolded it, "I hope it means that she has become more sensible. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>INDIGESTI9N IN WINTER. The Special Value of Bile Beans.</head>
        <p>INDIGESTI9N IN WINTER. The Special Value of Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>The cold weather is to be blamed for much of the stomach trouble prevalent just now. Cold depresses the vitality of the body; and this in turn hinders the digestion and assimilation of our food. Weak stomachs should be at once strengthened by that great vegetable tonic-aperient, Bile Beans. Mrs. Margaret Bishop, of 59, Banner Street, Waver- tree Liverpool, says : "1 suffered excruciating pains from indigestion and I was quite unable of rendering any assistance with the housework. My head and limbs ached terribly, and I felt thoroughly exhausted and jaded. When I got up in the morning, r felt that I had not had I sufficient rest ; in fact, I could not have felt more exhausted is I had stayed up the whole night. Illy, head ached terribly. I and upon the least exertion I suffered from palpitation. I I "To add to my troubles adisagreeable rash broke out 'on my forehead, and with one thing and another I thought I should never again have health and strength. I saw a doctor, but his medicine proved useless. "Looking through the newspapar I saw an announce- ment about Bile Beans. I tried a box, and my strength and health began to improve. I continued taking the Beans until the unsightly blotches on my face com- pletely disappeared, the indigestion ceased to trouble I me, palpitation became a thing of the past, and I could •I get up in the morning bright, cheerful and vigorous. I am now completely restored to health, thanks to taking Bile Beans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>UNKNOWN MAN DROWNED._</head>
        <p>UNKNOWN MAN DROWNED._</p>
        <p>On Monday at Runcorn, Mr. Dobson held an inquest on the body of an unknown:llan who was found drowned on Saturday in No. 8 basin, top lock. About 1 a.m. Frederick Hulse, a watchman, heard a cry for help, and went with a lifebnoy, but it was not until half an hour later that he could find any trace of the man. He saw something moving in the water, and called out for assistance. Ralph Wilkinson, captain of the flat Scythia, brought Mm a boathook, and he recovered the body. Wilkinson stated that on Friday night he gave the deceased per:o.BBton to sleep on his flat. He did not know him. That night` he assisted him with his flat, and while witness was at No 7 lock he heard a cry for help. The police stated that they had been unable to get the deceased identified. An open verdict was I returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LECTURE ON HAZLITT'S WORKS.</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON HAZLITT'S WORKS.</p>
        <p>The Influence of the Theatre.</p>
        <p>Continuing the course of University Extension lecture on English essayists at the Co-operative Hail on Friday evening, the Rev. W. Lawrence Schroeder, M.A. dealt with the works of William liazlitc. Ile pointed out that for six years Elazat was more or less regularly engaged in dramatic criticism. He liked the play and brought to its consideration qualities of mind which redeemed his critical work from mere common-place and gave some of it a permanent (place ,in the records of English literature. Having quoted Hazlitt's defini- tion of the high purpose which the stage should serve the lecturer said they should remember that even the third class theatres helped a certain class of society. It was curious that one's heart went out to a favourite actor and we liked to think that the man embodied in reality some of the noble passions he portrayed. Having pointee out that it was me literary side of dramatic work which attracted Hazlitt, the lecturer said that as la dramatic critic Hczlitt had certain solid I qualities which; commended him to our regard : a 1 power of keen observation for the niceties of ' expression, a real sympathy with the work of interpreta- tion—for he looked upon himself as an interpreter of other men's thought first—a faculty of judgment strengthened by a wide knowledge of the literary drama and purified by reflection and thought. In Hazlitt's time, Shakespeare reigned as king of the " boards "; nowadays at the best be was but a sovereign of the study. No one, he (the lecturer) took it, could quarrel with flazlitt on account of his point of view that the plays of Shakespeare were meant to be acted and in any consideration of their value that idea should receive i due attention. It might be that we appreciated Shakos- I peace best when we reflected on him, but after all the criterion of drama proper was the feasibility of the acting—whether the play could 'he made to live for the people. If it could not there must be something radi- cally wrong in the setting out of the plot. He (the lecturer) was not quite sure whether the increasing reverence for Shakespeare in the present century was absolutely the best kind of thing. It seemed as if we I were putting Shakespeare back and back until presently 'he would be one of the high gods untouchable. In Hazlitt's criticism, lack of scholarship was met by good sound common sense, and style compensated for an absence of deep and delicate searchings of heart. Hazlitt talked to us inc straightforward, fascinating manner. His miscellaneous essay work ranked among the best of its kind. One felt at times the superficiality of his I remarks but he said.' trivial things in a bright interest- ing way. His work was drenched in egotism and lacked the sweet charity that enlightened the essays of Lamb. We felt the work presented the . predominant characteristic of Hazlitt's curious personality—a certain harshness of nature and brilliance of powers. One of his chief characteristics was his virility. He compelled attention, and did not so much</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>THE...</head>
        <p>THE...</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE.</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXI.</p>
        <p>THERE was no sleep for the head of Scot. land Yard that night. . . . .. .. . . . ..</p>
        <p>As soon as he found himself back in his study at home, he set himself to work to collate his case and arrange his plan of cam- paign. First, with all deliberation, he discarded his fur coat and slipped on an old smoking jacket, frayed at the edges and full of associations. Then he filled a :arge meerscham, humming inconsequently all the while. Having lit his pipe to his satisfaction, he stirred the fire into a blaze and piled some more logs on. Last of all, he drew up a large arm-chair and put his leet up on the high fender. . .</p>
        <p>Thus settled in his favourite attitude for thinking, his thoughts began to take shape out of their previous chaos. "Lord," he muttered, "what a fool I've been—what fools we've all been, for that matter! It's as plain as a pikestaff, only it beggars belief! Who could or would have dared to dream or suggest it? Why, he'd have been laughed out of the country, clues or no clues! It's one of those things the mind refuses to grasp, even when its pushed under your nose. The Duke and Duchess of Hamp- stead—only one rung below the throne ! Well, well, it's all very well for old Horace to talk of a nil oclntirari policy, he ought to have lived in the twentieth century. And fancy a political plot in the twentieth cenfurv, an actual attempt to reincarnate a dead and gone cause! Why? Why, indeed? They all had their various reasons, I suppose, but it's mainly society with its jaded palate and perverted morals striving for a new sensation —trying, forsooth, for once to take itsell seriously ! Feminine enthusiasm and mascu- line boredom, the apotheosis of a neurotic craving for action and excitement, the evolu- tion of a fad into a phenomenon by a process hardly distinguishable, a summary reaction against the social round of modern life—the daily round without any common task. Some of the conspirators, of course, will prove to have had ulterior motives—personal gain, social advancement, and so on—with just an adventurer or two to set it rolling and to stir up the enthusiasm when it was wanted. It's a case of neglecta sofent incendla sutnerc vires. to go back to old Horace; who always fits the case. For years the embers of disloyalty have been allowed to smoulder, laughed at -with good natured ridicule. Each year the statue of Charles the First has been decorated by the various Jacobite societies, who have been simply regarded as harmless idiots with long memories and little sense. In 1902, for in- stance, they actually held an organised orgy in Richmond Park to celebrate the blessed memory of the good, horse Sorrel, that Jaco- bite samtwho two centuries before had thrown the usurper, King William 111., and thut made way for the last of the Stuarts in Queen Anne! A frolic in memory ,of a defunct quadruped is harmless enough, no doubt; but look at the way when Queen Victoria died they placarded. London with revolutionary notices, denying the right of King Edward VII. and proclaiming their Stuart princess who was herself on the best of terms with the so-called "usurpers ! " Well, well, it's nc good multiplyfing instances, but that's how it has been allowed to drift from mere fatuous- ness into the sensation of the century Cranks, no doubt, but nothing is more clan. gerous than a crank who begins to take him- self too seriously. Then he becomes fanatic, and God help the rest ! " • On these lines, puffing hard at his pipe. Sit Hugo reasoned out the genesis of the affair drawing his lips down grimly as he thought oi the consequences. . _ . .. •</p>
        <p>"What sa fool I have been I' he muttered again more savagely; "what an utter fool not to allow myself to realise the significance of the white roses ! Why, these conspirators started by giving themselves away by theix very emblem! If one could only have believe dit ! Why, the ' 'White Rose spells Stuart and Jacobites to the veriest board- school boy! But who could have believed ir a political plot in the twentieth century, especially with such an influential following i Either they were fearful fools—like myself— or else they were so wonderfully clever that they realised that no one would ever think ol putting the right construction on things as they stood! Now, how -do things stand ? I must marshal my points and my proofs for Colgate. Of course, Ben Nevis's words, though in delirium, are conclusive, and I'm glad that Lady Sabina, poor girl, was thoughtful enough to begin taking them down verbatim. But they only incriminate actually the Duke and Duchess of Hampstead, that scoundrel Branksea, and, this mysterious Mr. Stuart. I -wonder who form the rest of this precious committee ? "</p>
        <p>He thought for an instant. Then he jumped up and walked over to the corner ol the room, returning with a bundle of papers. "I've got it," he thought jubilantly. " Why, there was a long article in the 'Daily Reporter' of January 31st, dealing with the wreaths and the scene round King Charles's statue at the top of Whitehall." . ' •</p>
        <p>He found the' paper in question and turned to the social news. He carefully scanned the list, giving an occasional grunt, as he came across a name that particularly surprised him. Then with a pencil he made a full list.</p>
        <p>"After all., it's only suspicion against them at present, based on the fact that they fell in with a fatuous fashion. But some of them were pretty bold, flaunting their ill-omened white roses in the face of the public, hut their very boldness was a safeguard ita itself. Hallo, what's this ? " he exclaimed aloud, as his eye travelled further down the social gossip on the page. "Why, here is another piece of cumulative evidence. ' Last night the Duke and Duchess of Hampstead enter- tained at dinner at their mansion in I3elgrave Square the Marquis and Marchioness of Swanage, Viscount Branksea, Lady Mary Renfrew, the Earl of Struan, Viscount Step- ney, Lord Islington, the Earl of Portcullis, Colonel and Mrs. Mclver, Mr. and, Mrs. Stuart Mackenzie, Mr. and Mrs. Kno,t, Sir Alan. Atherley, Sir Napier Cordingly, Sir Andrew Wentworth, Mr. Reginald Carruthers. Mr. Ellis Gordon; the Master of Gareloch, Mr. William Brinckley, Mr. Godsmark, and Mr. lan Frazer.' By Jove, that's excellent, it seems to be the complete list, except for' Mr. Stuart,' who arrived later in the week, I expect. How annoyed the duchess must have been at the names getting into the paper I I suppose one of the servants makes a bit by reporting all the duchess's parties, if not her actual doings? Anyhow, that's something to go on with. The presence of Carruthers accounts for the enthusiasm of the 'Morning News' in misguiding the public. That Irish Member article was an excellent political red herring, worthy of the man. How he must have enjoyed the situation in his cool, cynical fashion!"</p>
        <p>Verschoyle got up from his chair and, -methodical by habit, replaced the papers, having Cut out the illuminating pieces from the ' Daily Reporter.' Then he sat down ,at his table and set all the facts he knew, 'Jig suspicions, and his deductions upon paper. At a quarter to four he had finished and roes with a yawn. " That will do for Colgate to show the King. He must have something to convince him with." He went over to the telephone in the corner and rung up Scotland Yard. " Tell Inspectors Belleville and Blew to be round at my house at six-thirty sharp, and Tin.' me up on my private telephone just before that time." Then he drew the couch up in front of the fire and threw himself upon it for a couple of hours' sleep, realising that he wonld probably feel the need of rest later in the day if he did not. From force of habit and power of will, despite the excitement of the night, he fell off to sleep and did not wake until the telephone sang. Having issued his orders, he oroceeded to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>6.•..... r. "Everything's fighting against us," said the duke impatiently, "and our only chance lies in kidnapping the King and anything Wentworth may be able to do in Half Moon Street." Branksea nodded in acquiescence. " You can rely on me this time," he said surlily. " I'll let nothing on God's earth stand in the way, and, though my leg may be crippled, I can drive a motor—to the devil, fi you like ! " Well, look here," went on his host, " this is my idea. On the excuse of your trying the new car preparatory to taking the King out on it I propose to go out this morning with you, taking Atherley and Gordon with us, so that you will all know the way on Sunday. They're to meet 'you there on Sunday and help You, so it's as well that you should all spy out the land first. The car's very fast, so it won't take long." " All right,"- said Branksea " I'm ready now, and it's just as well to take care that nothing miscarries next time." He rose as he spoke and swore again as his leg gave another twinge. " Curse my leg," he exclaimed angrily ; "it gets more painful every minute." "If it doesn't get better," said the duke sympathetically, " we'll wire to Wentworth to come and see it. He's the only safe man, - as Scotland Yard will he on the watch for a man with a bitten leg. By the way, how quiet they are keeping it ! They've quite cheated the public of their sensation.'" Just before one the car returned, and the four men, flushed with fast travelling, all felt better for their spin through the keen air. To their surprise they saw the duchess her- self on the steps, obviously awaiting them, end at once they began to fear something serii nts. Without a word she dragged the duke into his study. " It's all up," she said, breathing heavily, and looking ten years older. She handed him three telegrams. The first was from Sir John Kitcat, the King's secretary. " His Majesty confined to room with slight attack of influenza ; forced to postpone visit ; many regrets.—KlTCAS." "He may be ill," protested the duke feebly. "Read the. others," responded her, Grace, grimly. He (lid as he was told. " Overtures uncompromisingly repulsed; fear worst.—WENTWORTH."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>Ambulance Brigade Thanked. The following letter has been received by the Warrington Ambulance Corps from the Warrington Football Club :—" At the request of my Committee it , is my agreeable pleasure to bring to your notice the gratifying and most pleasing discharge of a duty to his fellow man performed by one of your Ambulance members of the name of Price. Not only did he render first aid assistance most satisfactorily but he magnani- mously stayed over night with one of our players I named Garside, who was taken seriously ill after the match, and acting under Dr. Collins' instructions had to be pat to bed as quickly as possible. Price unsel- fishly offered to attend to the player in question over !night, and I need not say how gladly his services were accepted, as Garside does not reside in Warrington, being a native of Manchester, and the journey home would probably have entailed serious [ consequences. Another of your Corps also assisted, but his name Ido not know. Garside feels his sense of gratitude to the courtesy and kindly attention , shown to him by the members of your Order, and at ' the same time please accept the best thanks of the IBoard of Managemeat of the above Club. With best , wishes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR. CUP FINAL.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR. CUP FINAL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lancashire County Union Com- mittee in Nlanchester on Tuesday evening, it was arranged that the re-played tie of ths Lancashire Senior Cup betwenn Leigh and Wigan should be played on the Broughton Rangers ground on Monday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>AWFUL RESULTS OF A BLOW. ---- Diseased Ankle for Four Years 1</head>
        <p>AWFUL RESULTS OF A BLOW. ---- Diseased Ankle for Four Years 1</p>
