      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. Warrington Win at Widnes.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. Warrington Win at Widnes.</p>
        <p>DROPSY, BRIGHT'S DISEASE AND HEART WEAKNESS. USABLE TO WORIZ FOR 32 WEEKS.</p>
        <p>Warrington placed a win to their credit at the expense of Widnes onl Saturday, and a:fair contingent of the club's supporters journeyed to the chemical town to witness the match. There would be about 3,000 spectatois when the teams lined out as follows : Widnes—Davies, back ; Wailing, I. Taylor, Peacock, , and Gill, three-quarter backs ;A. Taylor and Ditch- Mr. JOHN MACMILLAN, 15 Commercial Street, field, half backs ; G. Aspey, S. Aspey, Argent, McCabe, Bridgend, Perth, writes, Jane 30th, 1005 : "At the Lynch, Webster, McDonald, and Rigby, forwards, beginning of June, 1904, I got a severe chill on the Warringion—knight, back ; Widdeson, Isherwood, kidneys, and after a long attendance of two doctors, I Jordan, and OFlarris, three-quarter backs ; Brooks and was told it was Bright's disease. I had also great weak- Hockenhall, half backs ; Preston, Boardman, Thomas, flees of the heart, and suffered terribly from dropsy. Heath, Naylor, Hartley, Swift, and Harmer, forwards. When the first doctor stopped coming, we sent for Referee, Mr. J. Bruckshaw, Stockport. Touch judges, ! another, bitt he had not been attending me long, when Messrs. Gregory (Runcorn) and Shaw (Wigan). 'ho said there was not much hope ; so I stopped his The opening stages were somewhat tame, but eventu- treatment, and took Vase's SEAWEED TONIC. That ally some pretty passing was initiated for the visitors • was in February last; since then it has done for me by Brooks, and their efforts were at last rewarded by , what two doctors did not do in nearly a year. I w as- Widdeson getting over at the corner. Preston failed at ' off work 32 weeks, but I am now able to continue my the goal kick. Widnes were penalised, and Preston work daily, and lam about as well as ever I was, except placed a goal, but it was disallowed on account of one for a little swelling in the legs." of the Warrington men getting off-side. Half-time I VENO'S SEAWEED TONE' for purity and all-round score :—Warrington 1 try (3 points), Widnes nil. potency has not its equal anywhere for kidney troubles, The second half opened in favour of Widnes,but their stomach, liver and blood diseases ; it strengthens, efforts lacked finish. Warrington pressed, and Jordan regulates, and restores the body to a healthy and vigor- scored. flockenhall had no difficulty in placing a goal. ous state. Especially good for chronic constipation, lAt this stage Harris, Warrington, and Lynch, Widnes, indigestion, and all kidney and heart affections. Price, were ordered off for alleged foul play, and almost , Is. 11d, and 2s. 9d. at Chemists everywhere. immediately afterwards Argent (Widnes) and Hartley , (Warrington) also received their marching orders. ' Result :—Warrington 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points). Widnes nil.</p>
