        <p>LOCAL PLAYERS SUSPENDED. ! There were two unusual incidents at the meeting of At a meeting of the above league held in Nranchester the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. The first was occasioned by the Mayor's speech at the- on Tuesday evening, Broughton R tngers laid a charge against the Wigan club for, it was alleged, tampering Caledonian dinner last week in which he criticised with one of their players. After hearing the evidence, members of the Council. the Committee decided that Broughton Rangers were ! The MAvort having moved that the minutes of the quite justified in asking for an inquiry, though the previous meeting be confirmed, evidence was not conclusive enough to enter a convic- Councillor SToTT explained that he was absent from tion. The following local players were dealt with as the annual meeting of the Town Council on November follows :—Argent (Widnes) and Hartley (Warrington) 9, when the Mayor was re-elected, owing to indisposi- t suspended up to December 23 ; Harris (Warrington) to ion. He mentioned that fact because he did not wish December .31 ; Lynch (Widnes) to December 9. any misunderstanding to exist as to his actions. Ho was pleased with the remark his Worship made when he expressed the hope that the members would support 'him during the year, and thus do away with a great THE CONGO ATROCITIES. I deal of the bickering they had had. The members ! would, therefore, understand his surprise when he read the report of a speech delivered by the Mayor last Rev. H. Donnie° and the Baptist I week. •It had pained him to no little extent. What the Mayor had said he regarded not only as a personal Missionary Society. !insult to himself, but to sthe people who sent him there. Mavort : This is quite out of order. You must ! confirm the minutes, otherwise there can be no discus- ADDRESS BY THE REV. J. H. HARRIS. ; sion. I Councillor STOTr : Is it possible to move that any ; minute in the proceedings of the last meeting be not Golborne-s