cept your ruling, but I feel the same time to be King of the Belgians. King ; very much cut up about what you said. Leopold and half-a-dozen who were round him were The Mayou : Perhaps you have misunderstood what responsible for the extermination which was going on was said— of the people of the Congo. Therefore we had no Councillor STOTT : I feel it is disgraceful indeed for quarrel with the Belgian people, but as Christians and ; the Mayor of this borough to make such insulting humanitarians we had a (marred with King Leopold, and remarks.. (Chries of" Chair,"" Chair.") moreover with the system he had introduced— I Councillor WlLiussoN (indignantly) : Why should a system morally wicked and a system born in hell. Councillor Stott take this upon his shoulders any More After Stanley told the people of Europe of the riches than myself? It was not meant for you. to be found everywhere in the Congo every government The MAYOR : Never mind. and every country wanted a piece of land. Leopold Councillor Srorr : It was not meant for you. came forward and said "I am your man ? lam the The confirmation of the minutes was then agreed to. Heaven sent redeemer of the Congo,territory. Give me Councillor STorr : There ought to be an oppor- the territory and I pledge my word that I will carry on tunity given to reply to what the Ma.vou has said. good government there for the benefit on the one hand Councillor PEMBERTON : Do you definitely rule pf the natives and on the other hand of the commerce that -- of the world." The commerce of the Congo had beeia The MAYOR : You are qut of order. Sit down. Swept away and millions of natives had been rendered (Cries of " Chair," " Chair,"' Chair.") You shall keep hopeless and desperate. King Leopold obtained the your place. i Congo territory under a treaty n which the interests I Councillor Wilson rose evidently to speak on the of the natives and commerce of the country were safe Same subject, but the :Mayor ruled him out of order guarded. The riches lay in the forests and Leopold amid loud cries of" Chair ' and " Order." promptly claimed all forest land. To obtain the pro- The incident then closed. duce of the forests was the next question as the natives ABRUPT CLOSE OF THE MEETING. could not obtain the rubber as they had done in the past without stealing. In fact the natives were placed Councillor PEMBERTON had given notice that he would in the position of aliens in their own country. move :—(l) That the resolution of July 4th, 1905, A number of companies wee c••eated by accepting the tender of Messrs. James Simpson and Leopold who placed vast tracts of country under Son, Limited, for compounding the engine at Winwick the control of each Company on the condition that 50 ;be rescinded. (2) That any resolution in regard to per cent of the profits were handed to him. Still the spending any money on this engine (Water Lily) be question arose "flow are we to get the produce of the I rescinded. forests"? and Leopold's next step was to give the onus- I When the stage for the moving of the resolutions had panies the right to force the natives to obtain those arrived the MAYOII rose and said :—Councillor Pember- products —practically thereby enslaving them. He said ton's motions are out of order, and the business of the "If the natives do not bring sufficient rubber, take Council is at an end. some of them prisoners and hold them as hostages Councillor PEMBERTON : Will you explain how they until they are redeemed by their relatives." Each , are out of order ? company was given the right to employ hundreds of ' The MAYOR (leaving the chair) : They are out of black soldiers firmed with weapons of precision, to order. capture prisoners. Under such a diabolical system it Councillor LORD : If they are out of order why were was obvious to any intelligent man that atrocity upon they allowed to be put upon the agenda? atrocity and outrage upon outrage upon the natives were 1 The MAYOR (as he left the Council Chamber) : the inevitable outcome. It was in the taking of don't know. They are out of order. prisoners that the atrocities occurred. When the rubber of any village was incomplete twenty or thirty soldiers were sent to terrorise the people. This they did by killing and eating the natives, and pe rpert ra tin g indes- cribable atrocities. Mr. Harris proceeded to give specific instances of outrages committed by the black soldiers which had come under his own observation. When he went to his missionary station in the Congo there existed, some three days' journey distant a flourishing village. Hearing that the soldiers had been at the village he decided to visit it but on his arrival there found that it had ceased to exist, and that very few of the natives had been left alive. He was informed that upon entering one hut a soldier dis- covered a baby a few hours old. Seizing it by the feet he dashed its brains out and then shot the mother. Other details were given of shockin„e outrages which had been committed which, said Mr. Harris, were of such a character that if the people in England treated vermin in a similar manner they would be probably prosecuted and imprisoned. Some supposed Christians had been con- demning him for what he was doing in exposing what was going on in the Congo. They said he went to the Congo to do a Christian work and that which he was doing was outside his work.He made no apology for his action but said that if a missionary—a preacher of the Gospel of brotherly love--could stand by and See those things going on and not open his mouth to speak he was a traitor to God and to humanity. There was a man in Liverpool, Sir Alfred Jones, Counsel for the Congo, and President of the Liverpool Chamber of Commerce, who said if anyone went about the country as he (Mr. Harris) had done, " blackguarding people," he could not expect any favours. The missionaries did not want favours. They did not regard it as a favour to ask that what was going on in the Congo should be stopped,they regarded it as a Christian duty. Every Englishman and missionary who wore the decorations of King Leopold ought to tear them from his breast and fling them from him. Until they did so they could only expect criticism. He prayed that God would help every Christian in England to_do his duty in this matter.</p>
        <p>Some people will insist on doing things as done ye !rs and years ago, quite forgetting that great changes for the better have come about.</p>
        <p>Here is one great change:</p>
        <p>With Fels-Naptha a woman can do a day's work in about half the time and do it better.</p>
        <p>Although Fels-Naptha soap works so quickly it does not work with chemicals nor any- thing at all injurious.</p>
        <p>As Fels-Naptha soap is part naptha, which searches out dirt and grease, and part soap which .washes them off, it is entirely different from all other soaps.</p>
        <p>Mr. DuN.NIC.o said if the people of England,especially the members of Christian Churches,would only mike up their minds that Ow atrocities must cease they would be stopped within six months. We spent £250,000,000, and lost thousands of lives, professedly for the purpose of giving the vote to white men in South Africa, and we were doing nothing to stop all these abominable atrocities which were going on. He was almost ashamed to be a Baptist, because their Missionary Society was not quite clear in that matter. They might have done infinitely more than they had done, and he thought it was to their everlasting disgrace that they were the only great Missionary Society which had not joined the Congo lteform Association. He would be heartily glad when Mr. Baines retired, because he believed that had he not been at the head of the Society the letters sent home exposing the atrocities would have been given greater publicity. On the motion of the Hev.lll. Dumtco the following</p>
        <p>Being so different it must be used differently to get full results. See the simple directions.</p>
        <p>Of Grocers &amp;c., 21d. a bar. Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IVOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>IVOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Royal visit to Dorsetshire.—The King and Queen have always been on terms of intimate friendship with the family of the late Lord Alington, but until the Royal visit to Dorsetshire this week, the King had not been a guest at Crichel Hall, since 1891. His Majesty was to have spent a week there last December, but the visit was postponed on account of the death of Lady Alington's brother, the late Lord Hard wicke. Lady Alington is one of the most popular women in ultra- fashionable Society, and is noted for her somewhat magnificent style of dress. She has a great liking for heavy, rich materials, and being very tall and stately, she is seen in splendid costumes of velvet or brocade,to great advantage. Her daughter Miss Diane Start is one of the most sought-after girls in society, being very pretty, and possessing bright and entertaining ways, which make her a welcome guest at any social gathering. Society Poultry Fanciers.—The Crichel estate, which affords first rate shooting, is not far from Wimborne, and one of its features is the famous White Parm,where pigeons. fowls. cows. horses, and in fact every bird and animal is of pure white. A former Lady Alington was a great poultry fancier, and so else is the reigning Lady Alington, who still keeps no the fine collection of ban- tams which is a feature of the park at Crichel. Queen Alexandra also has a fanny for bantams,and is a frequent on re-winner at poultry shows, There are several well- known women who regularly annear in the list of ex- hibitorsst these shows. One of the most successful is Miss Sybil Amherst, daughter of Lord and Lady Amherst. and among other women ponitry fanciers may be mentioned the 'Marchioness of Salisbury, Lady Kathleen Pilkington. Airs. Millie of Dochfour, Lady Aylesford, and Lady Tichborne. The Portland Title and Estates.—The Kinea next country house visit is to he to the Duke and Duchess of Portland at Welheck Abbey, Notts. The Duchess of Portland has always been a favorite at Court, and -ttas one of the qnartette of" canopy " duchesses at King Edwsrd's coronation. His Maiesty and Queen Alexandra attended her wedding, and Queen Victoria</p>
        <p>VMS Godmother to her first child. It would appear that the title and estates of the Duke of Portland are to be the subject of renewed litigation, which for a long time centred around the nnsuccessful efforts of Mrs. Drnce to have a certain coffin in High- -1 gate Cemetery opened and investigated. The present Duke of Portland is the grandson of the third Duke, hut it is alleged that the fifth Duke married Elizabeth Crickner, whilst going under the name of Thomas Charles Druee. Their eldest son George Drnce died in 1815, leaving a son who claims the dukedorn,and entailed estates. It is contended that when the fifth Duke decided to drop his character, as Mr. Druce, there was a mock funeral and that the coffin in Highgate Cemetery. which is supposed to contain the remains of Thomas Charles Druc,e. really contains nothing but lead. The Blue Stone of December.—The turquoise reigns during the present month, and by many women whose birthdays fall in December, this pretty blue stone is worn exclusively. Princess Henry of Pleas, the Duchess of Marlborough, the Duchess of Roxburghe, Lady Lytton. Lady Dufferin, Lady Granby, Lady Minton and Lady Cunard,are noted for their magnificent collections of terquoises, while Lady Barrvmore, Lady Blois, Lady Hothfield, Lady Warncliffe, Lady Falmouth, Lady New. borough, Mrs. Adair,and Miss Violet Vanbrugh, all own beautiful sets of these particular gems. The best turquoises are a perfect blue, with scarcely any trace of green about them. They are however, particularly sensitive stones, and should never be worn by those isa bad or indifferent health. Under these circumstances, the bluest stones have been known to turn. green.</p>
        <p>The Care of Gems.—lf their proper brilliancy is to he ret lined all preci one stones must be kept, when not in wear, in sawdust, and the woman who possesses handsome rings should see to it that she removes them before washing her hands, that she never sleeps in them, and further that they pay a regular half-yearly visit to the jeweller for cleaning and inspection. Many a costly gem has been lost from a ring through the latter procedure being neglected. Opals require even more care than pearls, as they crack so easily and the surface is so apt to become scratched and marked. These gems should be repolished at intervals, or they deteriorate in value. The most sought after opals are</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHURCH CLUB LEAQUE. SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Sr. CLEMENTS T. PARISH CHURCH Y.M.A. (23n TEAMS).—Played at St. Clement's on Monday. Scores : ST. CLEM ENT'S. Y.M. A. .T. Fleming H. Broadhurst J. Hsmblett (capt.) T. Higham 75 F. Lunt P. Hankinson 75 S. Smith 3l Jesse Gray</p>
        <p>Majority for S. Clement' 34</p>
        <p>GOOD NEWS FOR EARLESTOWN. On Wednesday morning the London and North- Western Railway employs at the Viaduct Waggon Works, Earlestown, numbering some 1,250 started on full time, after having been on short time for some months. The Vulcan foundry locomotive building firm has also received orders for twenty more compound locomotives for the Buenos Ayres railway, which, with the work on hand, will keep the works running full time until the middle of next year. Last Christmas trade in Earlestown was very bad, but the improvements now recorded have given the greatest satisfaction to all.</p>
      </div>
