      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905. WOMAN'S REALM. SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS. DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905. WOMAN'S REALM. SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS. DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>those which have the greatest amount of red tint in their composition. Sequined Chi ff on.—Tn the world of trimmings sequined chiffon is claiming a foremost place, but it is to be doubted whether this idea is really a success, save as an interlining for ninon gowns. Ninon is in the greatest possible request, especially for young girls' evening dresses, and undoubtedly for such a purpose it possesses charms superior to those of chiffon. When carried out in white, the sequined chiffon is usually mounted over chine silk, the soft colours contained in the silk, and the glitter of the sequins shewing to the greatest possible advantage through the soft white over dress of ninon. Gold Trimmings.—For some little time wail, modistes I have been seeking inspiration from the fashions which were in vogue in France in the time of Louis XV., which accounts for the present craze for gold tassels I and buttons, gold thread.embroideries, gold galons' and I gold tissue trimmings. Provided this particular form of ornamentation is used sparingly, and by those whose good taste is beyond question, a touch of gold has much to recommend it, but usually the idea becomes all too quickly popular, when the well-dressed woman will have none of it. The Christmas Turkey.—Turkeys, it seems, are to be dearer than usual this Christmas. owing in a great measure to the disturbed state of Russia, and conse- quent large falling off in the supplies from that country. More than half our Christmas turkeys come from I abroad, and a large proportion of these have hitherto come from Russia. Many, no doubt, are sold as home- fed birds but the foreign turkeys are not so good. A large number of the birds sold in London come from Ireland, and are of good quality, but Norfolk has the reputation of producing the best turkeys. They are as nopular as ever, as the piece deresistance in Christmas fare&amp; but there are many who are inclined to think that the turkey is a somewhat over-rated bird, an opinion possibly induced by an unsought though too familiar acquaintance in recent years, with foreign birds of inferior quality. A Vegetarian Christmas Dinner.—Vegetarians are necessarily excluded from n large share of Christmas feasting, and are no doubt, all the better for it, but they manage to include many nice things in a Christmas dinner, of which a specimen was given the other day,by one of the London Vegetarian Socities at the Food Reform Restaurant. Many ladies were present, and women in fact now figure very prominently in the vegetarian movement. Lentil soup.and cheese fritters, were among the dishes, but the staple course was " nut meat roast." which took the place of the time-honoured turkey. This is prepared from thoroughly' cooked porridge and chestnuts, and may be eaten as it comes from the tins, or served as it savoury dish, flavoured with spices. Christmas pudding and mince pies are made with chopped nuts, instead of suet, and the whole repast of some eight courses, is served at a cost of only hal f-a-crown. .</p>
        <p>"ALA D GE.</p>
        <p>CALXIA.</p>
        <p>A new substitute for terra-cotta and plaster is a mixture of 45 parts of calcined gypsum, 30 of water, lof albumen. 9 of alum, 4 of sulphide of magnesia, and 2of borax. It is stronger and lighter than terra-cotta or plaster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WEDDING NOVELTY.</head>
        <p>A WEDDING NOVELTY.</p>
        <p>• While our weddings are, as a rule, passably pretty, we seldom strike a note of originality on such occasions in this country. In America it is different. At the recent Pulitzer wedding the happy couple were driven away from the church in an old-fashioned English coach with postillions and white horses such as one sees in the Gretna Green illustrations of the Christmas numbers. The postillions, by the way, were the two brides- men—for in the States the bride has men as well as maids in attendance—and these were gor- geously arrayed in red jackets, white trousers, yellow boots, and black peaked taps, as if they had walked out of a picture by Caldecott. They did not, of course, enter the church in that guise. There they were in the garb of modern civilisa- tion. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TRICKS OF THE Full' TRAM.</head>
        <p>TRICKS OF THE Full' TRAM.</p>
        <p>A friend wrote me lately in moving terms of indignation, says a writer in the Sketch, on the duplicity of a dealer in furs who sold her the skin of a 'tabby or unclassed animal for aristo- cratic ermine. This deceiver of confiding woman being left behind at Buda-Pesth, nothing in shape of redress was possible, and only un- availing lamentations remained. A moral may, however, be drawn from this touching tale, and the unwisdom of buying valuables like fur and jewellery from pnknown traders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SMART LACE COLLAR.</head>
        <p>SMART LACE COLLAR.</p>
        <p>The theme of the accompanying sketch is a dressy lace collar, which would be most effective for evening wear, and only needs a very plain and ordinary kind of blouse underneath. The foundation of our collar is a lace collarette, such as can bo bought at any price, from one-and- eleven upwards; round this is fulled a fine lace, about three and a-half or four inches in depth,</p>
        <p>a second frill of the lace being, added over eacn sleeve. A _flight of bows, either in some pretty colour or in black velvet, decorates the front where the fastenings are effected. It is rather a neat idea to turn the collar right round. in order that the fastehings may be down the hack. where they are, perhaps. not so noticeable as down the front.</p>
