      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TUE Nzw QUEEN'S HOBBY.</head>
        <p>TUE Nzw QUEEN'S HOBBY.</p>
        <p>Queen Maud of Norway has, like most other European RoyaltieS, her hobbies. Amongst the many things she has collected at various times is an interesting collection of ornaments and things made of ivory, of which she is a great admirer. She has, also, for years collected ivory tusks. Amongst the most precious trophies owned by Queen Maud are some tusks pre- sented to her by the King, which have been beautifully polished and fixed with small gold Oates bearing the date and a few particulars about the animal to which they belonged. Even more interesting than the collection itself are the manuscript notes and stories connected with it which the young Queen Maud has kept for many years and has bound in several volumes under the title "Tales of My Tusks."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>YOUTHFUL EDITOR.</head>
        <p>A YOUTHFUL EDITOR.</p>
        <p>A littWgranddaughtcr bf the ex-Lord Mayor of London has developed literary tastes, and with a number of schoolfellows published a magazine devoted to short stories. Ambition alone is not responsible for this entrance upon the field of literature. By the sale of the jour- nal she has been instrumental in forwarding a very welcome contribution to the British and Foreign Bible Society. It may be reniembered that the little lady, Miss Dorothy Allen, cut the birthday-cake this year at the juvenile meeting of the society at the Guildhall. I..7sEnt. IlonEsPuN DRESS.</p>
        <p>Homespun sounds a very unpretentious mate- rial, but though most inexpensive it can be made up into tho most becoming of indoor dresses. The soft grey of the natural fabric tones well with any room, giving the pleasing sense that the wearer is part of the room she is in. Our sketch shews a homespun dress so modelled that it can bo worn' indoors all winter, and pose as an out-of-door areas nett spring.</p>
        <p>The bodice is fulled over the shoulders, and drawn tightly down into a broad belt of black panne or miroir velvet. A tiny vest of tucked white erepc-de-chine edged with .bands of the velvet opens over a loose • front of the crepe, trimmed with medallions of lace. The sleeves end at the elbow with two frills of accordion. pleated silk, exactly matching the material in colour, the under-sleeves being of crow, to match the front. The skirt is full and long, and has two short straps, down the centre front. ill(' gathers over the hips being hidden beneath a tiny basque.</p>
