      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CLEANING WRINKLE.</head>
        <p>A CLEANING WRINKLE.</p>
        <p>I should be interested, says a writer in the Bystander, to learn if many other women, in daily intimacy with petrol, had discovered the invaluable service of this spirit as a cleaning medium. A gallon of petrol is thoroughly capable of renovating several silk blouses, which are just dipped bodily into the spirit and worked up and down without any rubbing. And my ex- perience has been that tinted lace has not changed its countenance, as one might reason- ably have expected it would have done. The evaporation is very rapid, so the odour dis- appears quite quickly. Nor need I add, I am sure, that the whole operation requires to be car- ried through in some place safely removed from fire or lights, else will another epitaph need to be written analogous to the one about the little boy, the gun, and the dust.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>• Scorcu EGGS.—BoiI three eggs for twenty minutes, then shell and put them in a basin of cold water. Remove the skins from 'lb. of pork sausage. Dip each egg in flour, then coat it over with a layer of sausage-meat. Next beat up one egg on a plate, brush each of the sausage-covered eggs over with it, then cover them with breadcrumbs. Have ready a .pan of frying fat. When a faint bluish smoke rises from it, put in the Scotch eggs and fry them a pretty golden brown. When the case of egg Atld crumbs has set it is well to lower the 11.4 kt muter me pan, or tne oursme wili DO 1,-.0 dark before the sausage-meat is sufficient)/ cooked. Drain well on kitchen paper, then cut each egg carefully in half. Place each half on n neat round of toast or fried bread, and garnish the dish with fried parsley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>INCOMBUSTIBLE CELLULOID.</head>
        <p>INCOMBUSTIBLE CELLULOID.</p>
        <p>Celluloid, hitherto barred from many uses be- cause of its explosive qualities, has been made incombustible through the invention of a French chemist. An ether-alcohol solution of celluloid is made, to which is added ether-alcohol of perchloride of iron. The two are mixed, and upon the evaporation of the solvents a com- pound having every property of celluloid, and which is not only non-inflammable but noncom- bustible, is obtained.</p>
        <p>PPIOTOGRAPHING THE NEUTRAL AXIS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Min igsberg, of Vienna, has devised a process for enabling the axis which is unaffected by strains when bodies are bent to be photo- graphed. The experiments can only be con- ducted with transparent substances. The bodies undergoing tests are placed between crossed Nicol's prisms. They are then photographed at various angles, and when the body is unstrained the dark ground remains unaltered, and, there- fore, appears entirely black; but if the body is subjected to strain it causes the ground to be- come partly bright, owing to the differences of refraction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LOOKING AHEAD.</head>
        <p>LOOKING AHEAD.</p>
        <p>When Asia, Africa, and South America have been as well studied as Europe and North America have been, says a writer on geology, there will probably be found vast stores of metals, coal, oil, and valuable minerals, thus adding to the world's stock of needful things. Also the discovery of new varieties of fossils. the ancestors of living species, especially of mankind, will add to the interest in human affairs. Geologists have for years been trying to find some definite measure for geologic epochs, to ascertain how long ago the glacial age was, and how long it lasted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>IMPROVED PILING.</head>
        <p>IMPROVED PILING.</p>
        <p>Concrete piles of an unusual form have re- cently been tested in New York. They are made by spreading a layer of concrete on wire fabric to which longitudinal rods are attached at intervals. The fabric is immediately rolled up in a special machine of simple construction, and the pile is then laid aside to harden. It thus contains any desired number of vertical rods in addition to the fabric, which, in a cross- section of the pile, has a spiral arrangement from the inside to the exterior of .the concrete. If desired. any one of the rods can be a hollow tube: so that the water-jet process may be used in sinking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ELECTRICITY AND PLANTS.</head>
        <p>ELECTRICITY AND PLANTS.</p>
        <p>How electricity influences plant growth has not been easy to explain. It is pointed out that the heat can have little effect, as this cannot replace or even greatly reinforce the heat of sunshine. The electricity acts rather as a stimulus to all the vital processes. Capillary attraction of water is promoted by a positive current flowing upwards. and as electric influ- ence is beneficial only with an abundance of water, the rise of fluid is assumed to play a part. In some exceptions a positive flow downwards gives the best results, which is thought to mean that more food is supplied by the leaves than is commonly supposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LEAGUES UNDER THE SEA.</head>
        <p>LEAGUES UNDER THE SEA.</p>
        <p>The bottom of the sea is an unknown region to most folk, yet it is an instructive and interest- ing subject which is well worth studying. To some extent it is comparable with the land, hav- ing plains, valleys, hills, a variety of soils, many climates, and special races of inhabitants. About 140 millions of square miles constitute this vast submarine space, and the average depth is 2,500 fathoms, or 15,000 ft.—a little less than the height of Mont Blanc. Many years elapsed be- fore scientists agreed that animal life of an abundant and varied character existed at the bottom of the sea at, depths formerly supposed to be quite or nearly devoid of life. The earliest known instance of living animals being brought up from great depths in the ocean occurred in the Arctic Expedition of 1818, when worms and a star-fish were obtained. There is now a con- sensus of opinion that animals of all the marine invertebrate classes, as well as fishes, exist over the ocean-bed, even to a depth of 4,000 fathoms.</p>
        <p>PHOSPHORUS MAKING BY ELECTRICITY. From an account of the process of producing phosphorus in the electric furnace, given in La Rcrac Mincralurgique, it would seem that this system of manufacture will Sooner or later re- place all other methods of preparing this sub- stance. The difficulty in the past has been to provide crucibles capable not only of resisting the extreme heat, but also the action of the sul- phuric acid, and much loss has been caused by breakages -Ind by the escape of the products in the gaseous condition. In the electric furnace the acid is not needed, and the phosphorus is obtained by fusing together bone-dust mixed with sand and wood charcoal. At the extreme temperatures of the electric arc the silicic acid of the sand combines with the calcium of the bones and liberates the phosphorus. This pro- cess, discovered by Welder, is only practicable at temperatures which render the use of clay crucibles impossible, us the evolution of phos- phorus only begins at I,lsodcg.Centigrade, and is completed between I,4oodeg. and I,soodeg. Centigrade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ALUMINIUM ALLOYS.</head>
        <p>ALUMINIUM ALLOYS.</p>
        <p>MagnaHum. an alloy of aluminium and magne- sium, is more readily oxidi,ed than the former metal on heating, but has a much greater tensile strength and Can be more easily turned. This alloy is made in three qualities, denoted by the letters X, Y, Z. all of which contain over 94 per cent, of aluminium. The first of these is used for castings where strength •is a primary consideration; the second is ordinarily used for casting; and the third for drawing and rolling, the latter operation being conducted at 300- 350 deg. C. Alloy X contains the following per- centage proportions of other metals—copper, 176; magnesium, 1'60; nickel, I'l6, with traces of antimony and Iron; Z contains tin, 315; copper, 0'21; magnesium, 1.58; lead, .072, with , a small amount of iron ; Y is intermediate in composition between the other two. Zisium and ziskon, two silver-white alloys of aluminium, make good castings and are used in scientific in- strument making; the former is the lighter, but : the latter is much the harder and stronger. Ziskon is a zinc-aluminium alloy, containing about one-third to one-fourth its weight of the former metal; it is very hard, resembling tool steel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>MAN IN THE MOON.</head>
        <p>THE MAN IN THE MOON.</p>
        <p>From the earliest days the irregular markings ,on the face of the moon attracted attention, and then, as now, all agree that they form the !features of a man ; and whether the moon waxes 'or wanes tho features remain the same. Ancient astronomers regarded the dark markings as seas, but the telescope laid bare that fallacy. In point of fact, says a contemporary, they are low-lying !plains of an enormous size, but are still styled seas by scientists. The two eyes are formed by ! the Sea of Showers and the Sea of Tranquillity; the Sea of Vapours, the Bay of Tides, and Mid- Moon Bay form the nose; and the wide and gap- ing mouth consists of the Sea of Clouds. The lunar mountain ranges shew, like those of the earth, a greater steepness on one side than on the other. The most remarkable of all the lunar features are the ring mountains, or great craters, , which irt-e very much larger than the biggest terrestrial crater. Under telescopic scrutiny the moon's surface presents many valleys, ravines, gorges, and clefts, some .of which are very sin- gular in character. Professor Proctor and other eminent scientists agree that the moon is both airless and waterless, so putt it cannot possibly he the abode of any forms of life resembling those with which we are familiar on this earth. COLOUR BLINDNESS.</p>
        <p>No exhaustive experiments have been made to ascertain the proportion of sufferers from colour blindness, but good authority tells us that one individual in thirty is partially, and ene ird)-irliini in i;,,ftr. is wholln. unable</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
