      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Nuisance at Penketh.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Nuisance at Penketh.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednes- day, when there were present :—Mr.Brooks (presiding), Dr. Murray, and Messrs. J. Ball, G. Thompson, G. Mather, J. W. Knowles. J. Backhouse, W. Houghton. J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), F. W. Kerfoot (inspector), B. S. Hewcombe (surveyor), H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), and Dr. Sephton (medical officer). -------</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ITE ALTIT OF. THE DISTRICT..</head>
        <p>THE ITE ALTIT OF. THE DISTRICT..</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPIITOX stated that during the month there had been 20 births, and 29 deaths. The death-rate was equal to 3113 per 1,000, as against 3200 for the corres- ponding period of last year. Of the deaths 21 occurred at the County Asylum, exclusive of which the death- rate proper for the district was equal to 8.6 per 1,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NU I S ANCE_ AT_ PENKETII.</head>
        <p>THE NU I S ANCE_ AT_ PENKETII.</p>
        <p>Mr. KERFOO-T—rep-o.r-ted that he had written to the Secretary of the Fleetwood Chemical Company inform- ing him that he had received a further complaint of the firm causing a nuisance at Penketh. lie had received a letter from the secretary of the Company in which he said he thought be should be told who it was who made the complaint referred to as residents in the immediate neighbourh)od found nothing to com- plain about. The firm were trying to carry out the undertaking with the Council and were expecting to remove to Widnes in ten or twelve weeks, their application to the Widnes Corporation having been granted. They were at present only drying material on the premises and were constructing a ventilating flue and fan by which means the fumes would be destroyed. He thought the Council should direct their attention to the nuisance-arising from a manure warf at Fiddlers Ferry. The previous Saturday the stench arising from the warf was so bad that be was made quite sick by it. . .</p>
        <p>Mr.Backhouse thought that with regard to the manure it should be discharged direct from the boats to the carts as was done at Sankey Bridges. Mr. KERFOOT said be was satisfied that no fresh material was going on to the Fleetwood Chemical Com- pany's Wr.,:qi.At present only drying was being carried on and that L. did not think constituted an offensive trade. The firm were doing their best to get off the premises. The matter was allowed to drop, it being understood that Mr. Kerfoot would take steps with regard to pre- venting any nuisance being created at the Fiddlers' Ferry Wad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. H BROADBENT.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. H BROADBENT.</p>
        <p>General regret was manifested on Saturday when the funeral of the late Mr. Harold Broadbent, whose death we recorded last week took place at Grappenhall church yard. Mr. Broadbent was highly respected in the dis- trict and at the funeral service which was held in St. Wilfrid's Church there was a large attendance. The ffi ociating clergymen were the Rev. Canon Willis (rector of Warrington),the H. V. Pigot (rector of Grappenhall) and the Rev. W. Sparling (vicar of Upton ; Chester). The following were the chief mourners :—Miss Broad- bent, Miss Sylvia Broadbent and Miss Lucy Broadbent (sisters), Mr. Tom Broadbent (brother), Sir John T. Brunner, Bart, M.P., (father-in-law), Lady Brunner (mother-in-law), Mr. J. Brunner and Mr. Roscoe Brunner. (brothers-in-law),Mr. E. H. Greg. sen., (uncle), Messrs. E.,11. Greg, jun.. Ernest Greg Robert, George and James Harvey, Miss Jessie Broadbent, Miss M. F. Harvey, Miss Margaret Harvey, and Mrs. .1. W. Side- botham (cousins), Mrs. Buckley, (sister-in-law), and Miss Clara Harvey and Miss Emily Harvey (aunts). Amongst others present were the Rev. G, C. Briggs,Dr. Elliott. Messrs. Mark Rathbone, W. Hutchinson, Gold, Holt, Coddington, George Harvey, Alfred Broadbent Nurse Hughes, and the servants (Mrs. Lownds, Sarah, Evans, Taylor, Ellis and Harriet). At the graveside were Mr. O. Jarmay (a director of Mes:rs. Brunner, Mond and Co., Ltd.,) his Honour Judge Bradbury (Bowdon), Mr. F. A. Frost, Captain Aupleton, Messrs. William, Frank and Charles Tinsley, Mr. Chas. White, Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Long, Mr. A. Howson, Mr. E. Gard- ner, Mr. Walter Milner (secretary), and Mr. William Carson (managing director of the Whiteoross Co.). The Liverpool Stock Exchange, of which the deceased was a member,vras represented by Mr. J. Phillips (secretary) Mr. 'l'. C. Morris (the deceased's partner), and Mr. E. C. Kendall. A large number of beautiful wreaths were placed on the grave.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRAMWAYS V SALFORD TRAMWAYS. —This match was played on Victoria Park Rangers' ground on Tuesday afternoon. The referree was Mr. J. Fish, the well-known Warrington three-quarter back. Alderman Smethurst, chairman of the Tramways Com- mittee, kicked off. The Warrington men at once assumed the aggressive and Knowles dribbled over and scored at the corner, the goal kick failing. Salford now had a turn on the offensive, but Warrington returned to the attack, and a fine passing bout by the borne backs resulted in Melia scoring. No goal resulted. At half- time the home team led by six points to nil. Restart- ing, Salford attacked and had hard lines on two or three occasions. From a scrimmage underneath the Warring- ton posts, Ricketts scored, and a goal followed. Result: —Warrington, six points; Salford, five. In the evening the players and a few friends met at the Lion note!, and sat down to a substantial dinner. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. H. Houghton.—Mr. W. Norton proposed the toast of" The Tramways Com- mittee, the Manager and the Electrical Engineer Mr. Teller responding.—The health of "The Salford Team " was drunk on the proposition of Mr. Mathias. —Mr. F. Fearneley, the captain of the Salford team, responded.—During the evening songs, gramaphone and Pianoforte selections were given, and a very pleasant evening was spent. The following contributed to the programme :—Messrs. J. Fish, E. Hanley, W. Speed, F. Fearneley, H. Clare, J. Melia, G. A. Knowles, J. McDonough, J. H. Knowles, A. ince, E. Atkinson, George Lomas, J. Howarth, and B. Bodle. , illunsonomosa Age</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>BABY COVERED WITH HUMOUR</head>
        <p>BABY COVERED WITH HUMOUR</p>
        <p>Eals Looked as if They Would Drop Off—Body Entirely Covered with . • Sores—Three Doctors Could Not Cure—Child Grew Worse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CURED BY CUTICURA IN TWO WEEKS</head>
        <p>CURED BY CUTICURA IN TWO WEEKS</p>
        <p>Mrs. George J. Steese, of 70/ Coburn St., Akron, Ohio, tells in the following letter of another of those remarkable cures of torturing, disfiguring skin humours daily made by Cuticura Remedies, after phy- sicians, and all else had failed: "I feel . it my duty to parents of other poor suf- fering babies to tell you what Cuticura has done for ray little daughter. She broke out all over her body with a hu- mour, and we used everything recom- mended, but without results. I called in three doctors, they all claimed they could help her, but she continued to grow worse. Her body was a mass of sores, her little face was being eaten away, and her ears looked as if they would drop off. Neighbours advised me to get Cuticura Soap and Ointment, and before I had used half of the cake of Cuticura Soap and the box of Cuticura Ointment, the sores had all healed, and my little one's face and body was as clear as a new-born babe's. I would not be without it again if it cost a fortune instead of the small sum it cost us to cure our baby, after spending much money on doctors and medicines without any benefit whatever."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>SLEEP FOR BABIES</head>
        <p>SLEEP FOR BABIES</p>
        <p>Rest for Tired, Fretted Mothers</p>
        <p>in Cuticura.</p>
        <p>Instant relief and refreshing sleep for skin-tortured babies, and rest for tired, fretted mothers, in warm baths with Cuti- cura Soap and gentle anointings with Cuticura Ointment, the great skin cure, and purest of emollients. A single set of Cuticura Soap and Ointment is often suffi- cient to cure the most distressing cases. Cuticura Soap, Ointment, and Pills arc sold throughout the world. Depots : London, tri Charterhouse Sq.; Paris,r. line de In Pair •, Australia. H. Towne a Co., Sydney. Potter Drug W Chem. Corp, Boston, Sole Props. 11137" Saud for "hew to Cure Baby Ilutuours."</p>
