      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>14; XA M TNER.</head>
        <p>THE 14; XA M TNER.</p>
        <p>DECRItBRR 9ch 1905.</p>
        <p>At last Mr BL- THE NEW MINISTRY. roux has resigned</p>
        <p>and left Sr HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN form a new ministry. This he is busy doin4---,vlth the uusouaht assist- ance of all the &amp;lily pipers. Wa regard with</p>
        <p>grave suspicion the numerous attempts at. minis- try-making which have been essayed by ths London i newspapers. There must, of course, be some hits among the nrunerous misses. Certain men are obviously destined for certain 091C99. We have however, no he'sitation in sayiog, oli the basis of our own information, that the new Government will not at all—even broa.4ly—co:Tespond to the surmises of the week. There is one matter of which little acsount has been taksa no to the present time, and that is the possibility that Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN will go to the House of Lords. He may go there either at once, or shortly after the General Election. S )me offices may be filled pr)visionally in view of this stop being delayed for a while, but it will not be long delayed. It is not advisable that the two leaders should be in the same House, more pirticularly at a time when the House of Lorcti contams 83 small a number of Liberal statesinon.</p>
        <p>But there is another and a more powerful reason. Sir HENRY CAMPBELL•BANNERMIN has a very long Parliamentary career. Be is not far short of being the Father of the House of Commons, He has occupied several high M u iste rial positiousand, it would be purely natural if he should desire to be relieved from the burden of Departmental work and left in a position to devote himself to the duties which especially pertain to the office of First Minister of the Crown. His elevation to the House of Lords, which is an event that must be looked for in the immediate fature,necessarily "que:rs the pitch"—if we may use BO vulgar but useful an ex- pression—of the earlier political prophets. Toe gratifying fact, so far, is that Sir HeNRY CAMPBELL-BANNERmaN has, with one exception, been able to secure the adhesion of all his former colleagues. So far from being a split, there is not even a small fissure in the Liberal ranks. Whether Lord ROSEBERY joins the new Government or whether he does not will depend on himself. He will no doubt be offered the opportunity, but we are hardly sanguine that he will accept it after what has recently happened. P., is needless to say that if he did accept office on the basis which satisfies Sir ho WARD GREY and Mr. Asonfrif, his eminent services would be welcomed by the Party.</p>
        <p>There were stories atioat on Wednesday as tl a visit paid by the Duke of DEVONSHIRE to the new Prime Minister. It was not surprising to find that they were speedily denied fron Devanshire, House. If such a visit had actually taken place it could not by any capable student of politics be supposed to mean more than congratulations. The Duke of DEVONSHIRE is a very shrewd politician. He is not at all likely to be led into forgetfulness of the fact that the new G uterntnent is unani- mously favourable to placing Irish etlairi in Irish hands, though the tubj3ct is not likely to be brought forward in the next Parliament as it was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905.</p>
        <p>in the Parliaments of 18S6 and 180. His views, being what they are, the Duke of DEVONSHIRE could not possibly identify himself with the new Administration to be formed by Sir HENRY CAMP- BELL-BANNERMAN. He may certainly, however be expected to give it a considerable amount of support, and he will probably command more anti-Protectionist votes in the House of Lords than will be found on the Conservative benches in the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>. Speculation as to who will be in the Ministry and who will be left out, what new men will be included, and what old men will be passed by or sent to " another place " on account of their age, is wholly premature. It may be taken for granted that Sir He Nair CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN will not form the whole of his Administration at a stroke. He will fill the chief offices, will present the list of his leading men to the King,and will be trusted to fill up the sub ordinate positions with a corres- ponding amount of wisdom. And he may be trusted above all in one matter. He will not take certain newspapers into his confidence before he has made known his intentions to his Sovereign, as was the case with Mr. BALFOUR—a course which as we understand, was very displeasing to the KING.</p>
        <p>The end has at last POLITICS—NATIONAL Coale Of "the strong- AND LOCAL eat government of</p>
        <p>modern times." After lagging superfluous on the political stage for many months during which its supineness has been ever-increasingly manifest, it has resigned like some feeble old man who knowing well that he was quite incapable of work was yet reluctant to relinquish a salaried trust. Mr. Balfour might have taken the plurem months earlier with inure credit to himself and his remnant of a party, and with less resultant national inconvenience.R.isigna- tions, dissolutions, and elections are not popular diversions at Christmas and the New Year. &amp;r Hoary Campbell-Bannerinan has courageously taken up the reins at a moment's notice and will probably be able to announce the composition of the new Cabinet on Monday. An appeal to the country for the necessary majority to transact Parliamentary business may his expected within the first fortnight of the New ! Year. Electors all over the country are burning ta give their verdict on the Government which abused its powers by plunging the country into a terrible war, peopling the Transvaal with Chinese slaves, and tampering with the fiscal policy which has for so long ensured the prosperity of the country. Locally there is not much to be done except to replenish the electioneering armoury, and both parties are busy with detail wotk. The Liberals are not letting the grass grow under their feet, and when the Liberal Candidate has been form- ally adopted the fight will be entered into with a' rcour altogether insprecalented in the elections of this last dozen years. Liberals everywhere realise that the long—sought opportunity has come. There' has been a steady rise in the party fortunes and now the flowing tide is with us to an extent possibly without parallel. The " Daily Mail," which usually steps in where angels and wise men fear to tread, is busy discussing the chances and pronouncing verdicts on the various prospective county and borough contests. Warrington was included in the list on Thursday, and our "whole- hogger representative was tipped to win again ! We are very glad—the tipsters are usually on the wrong side. According to the " Mr. Pierpoint will got in by a greatly reduced majority with the assistance of the Raman Catholics, " whose schools have benefited by the (Education) Act." If Mr. Pierpoint has to rely on the Catholic vote for• his return, his chances will be even less than we anticipated. The Catholics will not, by any means, vote solidly for the Tory Candidate in Warrington. Many of them will be influenced by the fiscal and Chinese labour questions ; and the one thing that we deem certain is that the Irish Catholics willfplump 4or the Liberal Home Rule nominee. Then ag 4in Mr Pierpoint's chances are largely discounted by the fact that there is a Free Trade split in his ranks, and for the further reason that many of his supporters are, like the nation, anxious for a change. Mr. Pierpoini, has been anything but an ideal representative even for his party, and that a ' largp propprtion of them will abstain from voting Is a moral certainty. They have nothing to plainp for whereas the Progressives have sortie of the strongest rallying cries which could possibly spur men to action. As Liberals we have only to put our shoulders to the wheel to record a triumph before which that of 1880 will pale.</p>
        <p>The R.Thabito</p>
        <p>AN ACTIVE TENIPERA.NCE worker 3 in the War- Onnsn. rington district were paid a well•deservad compliment by the High Chief Ruler of the Order, Bro. C. W. Churchill, Ohl Saturday, when he told them that if all the Districts in Lancashire had increased at the rate Warrington had done during the past fifteen years, the Oder would occupy a much stronger pasition in the county. The War- rington District has indeed done itielf credit in pro- moting the progress of the movero9ot, for the in- crease of membership during the period named was I from 296 to I,237—about 300 per cent. The work ; should receive a wimulous from the sound advice I which the High Chief Ruler addressed to those who are responsible for its direction. It is not as a friendly society that the claims of the order must be primarily urged but as apowerful organiz ttion in the cause of temperance. There are great possibilities for usefulness if all Rschabites will work with the unity of parpcse so strongly urged by Brother Churchill and well-wishers of the temperance movement will hope that this may be the outcome of Saturday's conference.</p>
        <p>The fact that it struck FAIR WAGES. a happy medium between the original proposal by Cmincillor Stott of a 21s. minimum, and the 184. commonly earned by the able-bodied labourer, was the principal reason why Councillor Barton's proposal was carried at the Council meeting. A minimum wage of 21s. for every able-bodied man employed by the Corrration is not in any sense an unreasonable demand, and the Council showed its appreciation of the necessity Ifor considering the social conditions of the workers by voting almost two to one in fav:.-mr of the motion, The Lahour men accepted it on the principle of half a loaf being better than no bre4d, aria if they would always display as much discre- tion in prilitics—we are not speaking person- ally—the Labour cause would make a more rapid advance than it , does. It is argued that 21a. is not a "living " wage for the married man with a family. We agree, but it is hardly the duty of the C-rporation to increase its minimum wage with the idea of encouraging emplc:qei to marry and bring up families. Such an Rim would, perhaps, be laudable but it is hardly a corporate duty. Twenty-one shillings per week as a minimum) for the able-bodied unskilled worker is as much as could, we think, he expected from a Corporation with the heavy rating burden that Warrington has. The wage-standard is sufficiently high to make a considerable d El9rence in the con- dition of thousands of Warrington workers if it were generally advted by manufacturers. It is riht for the C wporation to set the example, and ‘se hope that it will be speedily followed by all employers who take at iiitereat in the welfare of their employ 6,4 and desire to see an amelioration of the hard lot of the pi e'er olasses.</p>
        <p>We have on many A TOWN COUNCIL FARCE. (beef's inns qinpa- thiAi-d with his Worship the Mayor owing to the difficulty he has in COntrOqing It body INdliCb contrib.s so maoy unruly spirits as doeA the Two t..; mod. Hi has often been perfectly just;ilA in appealing for more orderly methods in debate awl strict observance of the SALnding Oilers relative thereto. NVe bi-lieve that the Comicill r as a whol«i sry desirous of conducting the business of the towa in an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>We must sooner or CONTINENTAL 'UNEMPLOYED. later accept a hint from the Conti- nental way of dealing with the unemployed. The Rev. W. Carlile makes a group of suggestions in the new "Nineteenth Century." He would have paupers segregated in separate buildings, and the cost of the whole thing shared between the locality and the nation, as some folks desire for that of education. The County Councils, too, should take over the present guardians' work, and in each newly apportioned district should be seven stations. Over all should be a Poor LAW Com- mission, in authority like the Prison Com- mission. The classification of stations should toe (a) for receiving all and sundry ; (b) worthy old people ; old and feeble ; (d) genuine out-of-works; (e) able-bodied loafers, vagrants, and thieves; (f) beggars, drunkards, and other degenerates; (y) the sick. Mr. Carlile's proposals possess another point of contact with the Education Act ; would accept the principle of Old-Age Pensions, and rule out popular election in the constitution of the authorities. So are our liberties departing ! Individual liberty, of course, would have to be sacrificed to the public weal. Oar roads and streets, as those of northern European countries are, would then tha cleared of tramps—who would devote themselves, instead, to land reclamation, afforestation, and agriculture. So would the national wealth be increased. Tie scheme might be tried piecemeal. The open-air labour restores a man bodily and morally, and, as at Frederiksoord in Holland, families might be removed from the towns and sottled on their own small holdings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Toryism costs a workMgman a week's wages for each member of his family every year. Liberalism is clieaper and more efficient. * * * . _ _</p>
        <p>Warrington is not going to be out-done by Stockton Heath in its tree-planting efforts. The first ceremony of the kind in the borough passed off successfully onl Saturday afternoon in Hazel-street and more trees wil be planted to-day (Saturday) off Knutsford-roacl. We shall have a Beautiful Warrington bye and bye ! *s* I The last ten years of Toryism has resulted in rates I for the Church schools, doles for the friends of the Government, taxes for the workingman, snubs for the volunteers, South Africa for the Chinese, cent per cent profits for the Rand mineowners, and (if Mr. Chamberlain had his way) dear food and dear goods for everybody. These are points to be remembered at election time. * * _ _ * .</p>
        <p>Local coachbuilders haveThot succeeded in getting the contract for more tramcar covers, though they made a resolute attempt. The work is, this time going to Heckmondwike, from which place an exceedingly favourable quotation has been obtained. The first lot cost S-80 each inclusive of electrical fittings. The four new ones will cost .£7O each inclusive of fittings. The committee is evidently learning where to lay out its money to the best advantage.</p>
        <p>Araong the bowling fri-ttern-ity of Warrington Mr. A. 11. Crostield is exceedingly popular and a very hearty reception was accorded to him at the annual dinner of the Warrington and District Bowling League, of which he is tbe president, on Thursday eveniUg. In a brief speech Mr. Crosield emphasised the value of games in moulding character but uttered a word of caution lest sportsmen in their enthnsia3m should allow games to" run away with them." By all means encourage sports but, as Mr. Crostield has said on more than one occasion, as a means to an end and not as an end in themselves.... .._</p>
        <p>" A merry midnight meeting " attracted a large crowd to llewsey Wesleyan Church on Saturday even- ing. This effort to promote teu.perance certainly bears the stamp • of originality. About " ctisinr, time " there was a torchlight procession, headed by a band, through the main streets, and many people who had just left the public-houses were induced to march to the church. Tee short service which was held. passed off quietly, the only untoward incidents being occasional interruptions from persons who were in an argumentative mood. We hope the new move- ment may have good effe3ts for greater activity in temperance work is sorely needed in Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Mayor's greatest gobby after posing A a humorist, is the cultivation if orchids, mid he has one of the finest collections in the country. On the occasion of her Majesty's birthday last week, Queen Alexandra was pleased to accept a box of the choicest blooms from the Mayor's hothouse. It should be added that His Worship by no means confines his acceptable gifts of these b3autiful and rare flowers to Royalty. He brings a supply to every Council meeting for the acceptance of the members. Some of them are apt to forget at times that they are wearing "the white flower of a blameless life," but that is not the Mayor's fault. * * * The'iMaycr's speech at the Caledonian dinner— the ratist remarkable he has yet delivered—was not allowed to pass withont a reference at\ the Council meeting. Councillor Stott, though ruled omit of order, succeeded in expressing the opinion that the Mayor's remarks were insulting in their application to some of his Councillors. It is a very curious thing that when he has any post-prandial utterances to make the Mayor turns upon the Town Council as a butt for his jests. If he has no better opinion of the Council than is apparent form these ill-advised utterances he can \ hardly expect much consideration from the memb3rs themselves. AUer Pleading for unity and amity so recently, the Miyor's retuarks were funny but in no other sense. The members of the Paving and Sewerage Com- mittee deserve both a Medal and a suit of oilskins in reward for their services last week. On that pouring Wbt Thuradav thay lve,.e out from 10.30 . a.m. till ,2 p.m. inspecting streets in various pal ts of the town. They chose an excellent time for their visit for every highway and byway that could hide itself mulct a small lake wss busily doing so. No wonder the mem- bers of that deputation feel a virtuous glow whenever street-pairing is mentioned. But they 'naturally re- onted the lowi (.omi-hints from Parr-street, Howley. Phir-street will have its turn. In the meautime the miniature yateht-raees on the puddles all )rd wet feet it unplanned ilehimihit —which are almost synonv• 11! —to the juvenile habitues of the neighbourhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy 'at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communiga, lions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington BACON IS DF.ABER.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S Lurie ToNic takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Coming Events. The second subscription concert for the season will be given by the Musical Society on the 20th inst. The programme will consist of "St. John's Eve "(Cowen) and miscellaneous items. The soloists will be Miss Hilda D' Angelis (soprano), Miss Nellie Anderton (contralto), Mr. Henry Brearley (tenor), and Mr. James Coleman (bass). Iron Fencing of every description. Lowest prices.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. The annual 'Warrington Eisteddfod and musical festival will be held in the Parr Hall on New Year's Day. InaOgurated four years ago, the event has proved a most popular and suecessful one, and the programme will be more attractive than in the past. The old features have been retained, and in addition several new contests have been arranged. Seats May now be booked at Wadsworth's, Saokey-street. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR, BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The annual choir festival in connection with Bold- street, Wesleyan Church will be held on Sunday. The preachers will be the Rev. J. Manning in the morning, and Rev. S. Pitt in the even ing.A service will be held in the afternoon at which selections will be given by Messrs. Crosfield's bond, and solos by Mr. Arthur Weber of the Liverpool Cathedral. On Monday 'evening the cantata " Tho Holy City" will be given by the combined choirs of Penketli and Bold-street Churches. B‘ACCN IS DEARER.—Advt. - An open meeting of the Wycliffe P S.A. Society will be held on Sunday afternoon. ANlerman Tinnion will preside and an address will he given by the ,Rev. 11. H. Sewell, of Liverpool. " Do the dead live?" is a sabject which will be debated in the Conservative Chtb Assembly Room next Tuesday and Wednesday by Mr. E. Marklew, spiritualist, and Mr. P. IL Ward. secularist. On Tuesday the Rev. J. F.. Matthews will preside and the following evening the chair will be occupied .by the Rev. C. Harvey Cook. T. E. WHITE, 22, Sankey Street for Silk Mufflers and Ties for XMAS PaEsEvrs.—Advt. Notice. Owing to the great pressure on our soca cans eel by Christmas advertising we have been obliged to omit several items of local news. CHEESE IS DEARER.—Advt. CiinrsTm AS PRESENTS,—Dressing-Caseq, Umbrellas and Gloves.—T. E. WR ITE, 22, Sankey St.—Advt. St. John's Sale of Work. The annual sale of work in connection with St John's Presbyterian Church was opened in the schlol- room, John-street:Yon Thursday:by Miss Dutton. The sale is in aid of the Church expenses. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) UNDER TM: CLOCK for GoocipooTS.-4dvt. Death of Mrs. Francomb. We record with regret the death, which took place at her residence at Wilderspool on Wednesday, of Mrs.' Francomb, wife of Alderman Francemb. The deceased lady, who was 59 years of age had been in a delicate state of health for sometime. Sherwas held in high esteem and in the years 1859-90-91 discharged the duties of Atiy oress of the Borough with grace and distinction. The funeral will take place at the War- yington C,einetery on Monday, Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, Lamps, &amp;c.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLA.Sb 1111NERAL WATERS.—AdVt. Death of Mrs. Perris. . Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Perris, wife of Mr Alfred Perris, 137, Sankey- street, who passed away somewhat suddenly on Saturday, at the age of sixty-one years. The deceased , had been in a weak state of health for a considerable period, and her condition became more serious as the result of an accident in September, 1904 when in alighting front a train at Bank Quay station she slipped and fell to the platform. She apparently recovered from the sh,ck but recently had a relapse and gradually sank. She was highly respected by a large circle of friends and much sympathy is felt with Mr. Perris and family in their bereavement. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday, afternoon, the Rev. H. S. •Perris (London) officiating. The chief mourners were Mr. Alfted Perris (widower), Mr. Alfred Perris (son), Mr. G. W. Perris (brother-in-law), Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Ward (brother and sister-in-law), Mr. a. A. Ward (brother), and Mr. and Mrs. F. Vogt. Amongst others who attended were Mr. and Mrs. Hill, Mrs. Madeley, Mrs. Boneliill, Miss Liptrott, and. Miss Lawton.</p>
        <p>Galvanized Roofing Sheets, ltoofing Volta, 'Large stock.—J.S. GARLAND, 51 to 53, Bridge at., Warrington.—Adrt.</p>
        <p>A NEW BISCUIT FOR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Madame Clara Butt's Visit.</head>
        <p>Madame Clara Butt's Visit.</p>
        <p>Oar readers are reminded that Madame Clara Butt (Mrs. Kennerley Rumford) and her husband and party will pay anothar visit to Warrington on Wednesday evening next, and give a concert at the Parr Hall. They will be assisted by Miss Pauline and Ethel Hook (vocal duets), Mons. Tivadar Nachez (solo violin), Mr. W. H. Squire (solo violoncello),and Mr S. Liddle (con- ducto'r). Tall and graceful, an exemplification of the beauty of contour, Madame Butt is an ornament to any'. stage ; but it is the rich and cultured voice of which she is the fortunate possessor rather than her charm- ing figure that has made her so popular. Mr. Kennerley Rumford has also made a big reputation for himself on the concert platform. A most attrac- tive programme is being provided and it may be in- teresting to note that Madame Butt will sing aria, " 0 Don Fatale " (" Don Carlos "), (Verdi) ; new songs " Cossack cradle song" (Charles Baughan). ; " Eng- land, Mother England" (E. Booth) ; " For love of you " (E. Newton). The plan of seats is now open at Mr. Pearson's Bold-street, and those who intend to be present at the concert should secure their tickets as early as possible.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. TINDER TEIE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Christmas Presents, Splendid New Stock.—J. S. GARLAND. 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. P.S.A. Society Meetings. There was a large attendance at the monthly meeting of the Buckley-street Society which was held on S in- thy. In the absence of Mr. J. Stringer, the speaker was Mr. H. Woolley,who based his remarks on "There is a way which seemeth right, but the end thereof is ! death." The Warrington Orpheus Quartet were in attendance and rendered the 150th Psalm, the music being composed by Mr. T. Pemberton. an inmate of the Warrington Union Workhouse. The Quartet also ! attributed "God is a spirit " in a praiseworthy manner. The accommpanists were Mr. A. Trow and Miss M. Plinston. Mr. E. Sanders read the lesson. lAt the Warrington P.S.A. meeting Mr. J. H. Holland who presided pointed\ out the great need there was for more whole-heartedness in all Christian effort, and for more sympathy amongst Christian workers. In absence of the appointed speaker, the President (Mr. E. W. Jones) gave the address, and drew attention to the admirable speech delivered recently in Warrington by the Bishop of Liverpool. The Bishop stated that "The church is morally responsilole for the character of the com- munity in which she is planted." He (Ur. Jones) believed that if error, misery, wretchedness, sin, intemperance, and impurity, were overcome, it would be by the effort put forth by the Christian church. Mr. Warburton rendered two solos " Seek ye the Lord" and "Thou art passing hence." Mr. B. Wadsworth was the accompanist. Mr. J. D. Gibson read the Scripture lesson, and Mr. R. Nelson and Mr. T. Taker took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUMNDICIFLA.M'S HIGIFE.-CLAS4 MINKECAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>170-to-date Fire Places.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Popular Penny Concerts.</head>
        <p>Popular Penny Concerts.</p>
        <p>That the popular penny concerts are being widely appreciated was evident from the very large audience which assembled in the Bold-street schools for Satur- day evening's entertainment. The programme was one of great attractiveness and was provided in its entirety by Mr. T. Brough's juvenile choir. It is duo to the children to say that they acquitted themselves most admirably and special praise is due to Mr. T. Brough upon whom the work of instruction had de- volved. The choruses " Comrades' song of hope," " Excelsior " and" Sister Elves," and the part song "There's a Friend for little children" were given with fine effect, fall attention being paid to expression. Items by individual members of the choir were highly creditable. F. Bennett gave " Kentucky home" and " Beyond " and A. Dutton sang" A horse." A feature which created considerable merriment was the song " I'm tired" by J. ;MITA whose actions were in every way suited to the words, and a hearty encore was accorded. Other interesting features were the duets" When We are married " by E. Dann and T. Tanner, " There ain't another daddy in the world like mine " by P, Barton and A. Brough and " Life's Dream is o'er" by P. Barton and A. Brough. Six boys and girls gave a successful interpretation of" Your future husband." Miss Atherton contributed a piano- forte solo and acted as accompanist. The concert was presided over by Mr. E. Plinston who delivered a short address. A miscellaneous programme will be sub- mitted at to-night's concert, by Mrs. Coulton, Ur. Clay, Miss Garnett Ball, Mr. Davenport,Urs. Winifred Pigot, Miss Amy Pigat and a Quartette Party.</p>
        <p>LADTES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK. BRMON-STREET.—Advt. Potrnlenrn Oil in bsrrels and drums delivered free.—.T. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, bridge et., Warring- ton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL SENSATIONS</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL SENSATIONS</p>
        <p>THE COMPOUNDING QUESTION. Appeal to the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>SIGNED BY A MAJORITY OF THE COMM.</p>
        <p>A. strongly worded letter has been sett o the Secretary of the Load Government Board objecting to the Board's saltation being given to the 'loan of ;!:.1,0(X) for the purpose of compounding the old beam engine at Winivick. The letter is signed by a majority of the members of the Council, and repre- sentatives of influential local associations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Special Council Meeting to be Called.</head>
        <p>Special Council Meeting to be Called.</p>
        <p>Eighteen Signatures.</p>
        <p>A. requisition T 135 been forwarded to the Mayor. requesting him to call a special meeting of the Council at which the business will be " (1) to' rescind the minute of the Water Committee confirmed on July 4th accepting the tender of Messrs. James Simpson and Son, Ltd., for the compounding of the beam engine at Winwick for £1,530; and (2) to discuss the position I of the Corporation with reference to the application to the Local Government Board for their sanction to borrow £1,003 for the carrying out of the work, and 'to move such resohtgons as the Council may deem !necessary."</p>
        <p>The requisition has been signel by Alderman l'ierpoint, Councillors Duncan, Chantler, Joseph, Wilson, ,Benson, • Hulse, Wright, Burton, Barton, .Ashcroft, Forshatv, Roberts, Dickenson, Stott, Maginn, Lord and Pemberton.</p>
      </div>
