      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FORESTERS' DINNER. SPEECHES BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. Alderman Tinnion and the Mayor.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' DINNER. SPEECHES BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. Alderman Tinnion and the Mayor.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering of Foresters at the Lion Hotel on Saturday evening, when the annual dinner in ccnnection with the Warrinzton District of the Order was held. This is only the second occasion on which a united social gathering of Warrington Foresters has been held, but the proceeedings were of a most success- ful character. About 200 members sat down to an excellent repast which was served by Mr. John Lee. The post-prandial proceedings were of a highly enjoy- able character and were under the presidency of Bro. P. Peacock, D.C.R., who was supported by Bros. A. H. Crosfield, C. Roddy, G.H.C.R., of the Irish National Foresters, .1. Owen, S.D.C.R., L. Davies, D.S., J. Bancroft and J. Walsh, District Trustees, P. Britnelow, P. R.°. H. Maybury, J. Chrimes. H. James. W. Hankiuson, G. May'briry. R. Tbornley, H. Hill, A. Tilling, W. Hip- kiss. P.D. C.Rs., J. Davies. W. Smith, T. Smith, P. Welding, E. Clayton, G. H. Fearnley, D.C.R.'s. com- mittee of management, Alderman Tinnion, Captains Appleton and Smith, Bro. Hulse, Chester, P.H.C.R., High Court Trustee,Bro. Pownall, Altrinchamond Bro. Latham, Earlestown. Letters of apology for non-attendance were read from Bro.Smith,chairnuin of the North-Western Federa- tion, Bro. Stead, permanent secretary of the Order, and Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., who wrote from Dax, France,regretting that the illness of Mrs.Pierpoint pre- vented his accepting the invitation. THE TOAST LIST.</p>
        <p>- - - - The usual loyal toasts were honoured on the pro- position of the CHAtuat.ol. Bro. SMITH, P.D.C.8., submitted the toast of the Army and Navy a toast which was heartily drunk. C.iptain APPLETON in responding said that with regard to the navy he thought they must all admit that the " Jack Tar "of to-day was the same as he was in the old days. He believed the officers were as capable and were, perhaps onestentatiously,doing their duty as man- folly as they did in the days of Nelson. The volunteers also did not forget the oak from which they had sprung (Applause.) He would never forget the kindness which be had received from Warrington Foresters, and he begged their acceptance of a cigar lighter, made from an old shell which he picked up on the South African vett. (Applause.) On behalf of the Order Bro. Peacock expressed his hearty acceptance of the gift. Captain SMITH also responded to the toast of the Army and Navy. In regard to the volunteers, he said some battalions had become very much more efficient than others, and the Warrington battalion was classed amongst the most efficient in the country. (Hear, hear.) That week a climax had been reached by reason of an order issued by the War Office, with regard to different rates of pay which were to be given in the future to volunteers. On paper the order looked all right, but when they came to examine it they found that to the Warrington Battalion it would mean an annual decrease in the income of between .200 and £6OO. (" Shame.") Althouet the Warrington Volunteers were amongst the most efficient in the country they were to be " docked " £6OO. He hoped the order would never become law because if it did it would be very dirlicult for the Colonel of the Wart ington Volunteers to carry on the Battalion. (Applause.) "THE ORDER."</p>
        <p>- - Mr. A. If. CROSFIELD, who on rising to speak, was greeted with a prolonged outburst of applause, sub- mitted the toast of " The Ancient Order of Foresters." In proposing the most important toast. of the evening.be said,he was sure be only expressed their sentiments and hearty feelings when he asked to he allowed to convey a message to the Member for the Borough, telling Hm how truly sorry they were for the circumstance which bad delayed him on the continent. (Applause). lie trusted Mrs. Pierpoint would soon be completely restored, and that they might have their Member back amongst them. (Applause.) Us wanted very briefly to read them a few figures as showing the marvellous derelopment of the Ancient Order of Foresters. Roughly</p>
      </div>
