        <p>Bro. C. RODDY briefly responded, and io doing so urged the great value of Friendly Societies.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO BRO. lIIPKISS.</p>
        <p>Bro. G. NIAYIII7I2Y on behalf of the Warrington Dis- trict of the Order then presented a beautiful framed emblem of the Order in recognition of his services during the past year as District Chief Ranger. He spoke in high terms of Bro. liipkiss' interest in and work on behalf of the Order and remarked that his year of office as District Chief Ranger had been an unique one in the history of the District. The annual dinner and summer pic-nic were inaugurated and in addition the annual conference of the North-Western Federation was held at Warrington during the past year. (Applause). Bro. HieKrss, who upon rising to return thanks was greeted with applause, spoke of the valuable assistance which had been given to him ny his brother officers of the District and expressed the hope that Bro. Peacock would receive the same support. (Applause.) . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>During the evening musical items were given by Messrs, J. Willett, P. J. George, the Lyric Prize Quartette, the Misses Woodland and Fairhurst and Messrs. George A. Cook, and Mr. Rutter (cornet solos) Humorous selections were given in a highly capable manner by Mr. Arthur Maybury.</p>
