      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>REVISION OF ELECTRICITY CHARGES.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- cil was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday when there were present :—The Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Alder- men Fairclough, A. Bennett, John Evans, J. Smethurst, D. Tinnion, Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, I'. Barton, W. Benson, W. Brough, T. Burton, T. Chantler, W. Clegg, J. Dickenson, James Evans, L. Ellison, T. J. Hulse, J Lord, J. Maginn, J. Pemberton, .T. Sankey, J. Skelland, J. F. Smith, F. Stott, J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson, and T. Wright, and Messrs. 3. L. Whittle (town clerk), A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough. surveyor), J. Dens, (water engineer), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), F. V. L. Mathias (electrical engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CONDITION OF THE STREETS.</head>
        <p>THE CONDITION OF THE STREETS.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee said the committee had decided to proceed with the new road from Wilderspool Causeway to Knataford-road. He thought the road would be of the greatest possible benefit to residents in the neighbourhood. Alderman TINNIoN : Have 1 to understand that the committee have decided to proceed with the work Alderman BENNIffr : I meant to say that plans have been approved. The minutes were approved. .</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee. Alderman EVANS asked if the attention of the com- mittee had been called to the state of Parr-street.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION said that on Thursday of last week a sub-committee started out at half-past ten, and were out till two o'clock inspecting certain streets' but they had not got as far round as Howley Ward. However, in the morning they were starting out again and hoped to got to Howley and Latchford Wards. After they had completed their examination the next thing would be an application to the Local Government Board to borrow probably £50,000 on private street works. This would not come out of the rates, but from the property owners. The committee were doing all that was humanly possibly, to cope with the many new streets recently added to the borough. The condition of ; Parr-street was not due to the committee. They had scheduled the street, anc' applied to the Local Govern- ment Board for power to borrow the money. As soon as sanction was received, and there was reasonable weather, the work would be proceeded with. IThe minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>COUNCILLOR LORD'S QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR LORD'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>CJancillor Lour) asked the Chairman of the Water Committee (1) If application had been made to the Local Government Board for powers to borrow the turn of £23,510 for waterworks purposes as passed by the Council on January 3rd last? (2) If se,on what date was the application made? (3) If it had not been mtde, why not</p>
        <p>--Alderman EVANS said that the anenrer to the first, question was " No." With regard to the third query it was intended to ascermin the exact amount required to be borrowed by reference to arbitration, but this had not yet been done.</p>
        <p>ELECTRICITY AND TRAMWAYS COMMITTEE. REVISED CHARGES FOR POWER. The Elecricity and Tramways Committee recommended that the following scale of charges for electric energy supplied for power be adopted:—Load far;tor(per cent.), 40, charge per unit, Id. : 50, Old. ; 60, 88d. • 70, 82d. ; 80, 79d. ; 90, 701 ; intermediate values of load factor being worked out proportionately. They also recom- mended that the tender of Messrs. W. Rouse and Sons, of Heckmondwike,for four car top covers at £279 18s. 6d. be accepted. Alderman SMETHURST in moving the adoption of the minutes, said that with regard to the tender for four car top covers, the committee were very anxious that one of their own townsmen should have the job and gave them every chance. Unfortnnately, the tender for the local firm was for .C3oo,which wasconsiderably higher than the accepted tender. With regard to the scale of charges for electric energy the engineer had forwarded him is letter, which read as follow :—" Dear Sir,--I notice that in the agenda for the Council meeting, the j Town Clerk has left out the clause, That this scale of ' charges be based on the present prices of coal, and might be varied in accordance with the cost of coal. ! The committee found it necessary to introduce a new Iscale of charges for power, as there are now some con- sumers who are continuous users of power ; the cost of ! production of whose power therefore costs the depart- merit less. To the non-technical, it may appear that the charges for electrical energy should be I equal; this view, however, is entirely erroneous. The I cost of production is dependent on load factor, it there- fore follows that consumers with a high load-factor I should be encouraged, and be chargod at a price on which an equivalent profit is made to that on other con- sumers. This scale of charges has been worked out on these lines. For want of some such scale of charges , the department have already lost consumers, and are i likely to lose more ; on the other hand with a scale of ' this kind, business is sure to accrue." Councillor Asncnore asked why Mc Dowell's tender had not been accepted. It was only £2O higher and he thought the tenders should have gone to a local firm. Councillor LORD said that the next tender to that accepted was one of a local firm, but it did not pro- vide for the electrical fittings. They wanted a little longer time, and as the Council had played with the matter all the summer,he thought they should have been prepared to extend the time, if they could have kept the order in the town. He understood the cost of electrical fittings was considerable. Merms. Rouse had undertaken to provide these fittings in their contract. . . . .</p>
        <p>The MAYOR explained that the-next lowest tender which included electrical fittings was Messrs.Milneand Co Birkenhead, which was for £3BO. . .</p>
        <p>Alderman Tim:Kw said that there was also the fact I that the tender of Messrs. McDowell was not according to specification. The patent window ventilation was altogether different. The side windows, according to this firm's plans, were fixtures and the ventilation would consequently have to be obtained from end to end. The committee had given this matter careful consideration, and had been desirous of giving the work , to a local firm. Referring to the alteration in charges ! for electric power, he said he hoped that some means would be taken to make known the very low figure which they charged for motive power. The Committee had been compelled to reduce the cost, not only to save oldcustomers, but in order to induce them to take more power from the Corporation. He hoped that many who had hitherto fought somewhat shy of using electricity as a motive power would re•consider the matter, and that this reduced charge would lead to them making the ex eriinent. Councillor STOTT inquired if the account of £B3 2s. Bd. in connection with the tramways department, included the wages of a conductor, who had been sus pended four or five weeks ago when a charge was made against him and he was given the option of being prosecuted or dismissed. The man decided to be prose- cuted, but he had neither been prosecuted or dismissed. If he was guilty of the charge, which had been made against him, he was entitled to no wages at all. If he was not guilty be was entitled to go on with his work. Alderman lIHNNETT congratulated the committee on the enterprise they had had shown in reducing their charges for electrical power. Everything should be done to encourage the manufacturer. He was particu- larly glad that the trend of the resolution was a step along the road which led to blue skies. Aldermen SMETHURST said that the matter with , regard to the tram conductor was in the hands of the ! teenager and the Town Clerk. Consequently, he would not say anything about it, and would leave it there. Councillor LORD: The chairman of the committee has not answered Councillor Stott's question. The Mareu : Ile has in his own way. (Laughter.) The minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CORPORATION EMPLOYEES' WAGES.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION EMPLOYEES' WAGES.</p>
        <p>21s. MINIMUM AGREED TO. ---- . What is a Living Wage?</p>
        <p>The question of the wages paid to Corporation work- men was again under discussion at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening and a minimum of 215. per week for able-bodied employees was approved. Councillor Barton introduced a motion in the following terms:—" That the following motions become Standing Orders of the Council :—(1) No able- bodied man employed under the Corporation and hiving reached 21 years of age shall be paid less than at the rate of twenty-one shillings per week. (2) That each able-bodied man with two years' service or more be given one week's holiday each tyear. The above resolutions to come into force on or as soon after the Ist January next as practicable." He moved the first clause of the resolution separately. He pointed out that some few months ago the question of a minimum wage was discussed and he thea stated that if the scheme had been a more moderate one he would have willingly supported it. He believed the resolution which he now proposed was a very fair one and he hopad all the members of the Council would support it. He did not say that the Corporation should be model employers but they ought not to be behind others. As the wages were paid from the rates they should pay the workmen a sum sufficient to enable them to live respectably and comfortably with their families. Councillor DICKENSON seconded, and said the resolu- tion was a very fair and broad-minted one. The amount was not much when one took:into consideration rent, light, fire, clothes and school pence. Councillor ASHCROFT supported, and said that most Corporations were moving in this matter,Liverpool and Manchester for instance. In Liverpool 245. was paid, although he admitted that in Warrington the rents were not so high as they were in Liverpool. There were some able-bodied men who worked for local firms for 17s. 10d. and 18. a week, but anyone of sense knew that that was only a bare pittance. 'What was proposed would mean about 210 a week increase in thelwages bill. Councillor STorr said lie would support the resolu- tion Although he was not satisfied with the amount proposed. The sum named was extremely moderate, and he contended that the Corporation ought to be model employers and set a mark for other employers of labour to aim at. Did Councillor Barton consider that he could live on 21s. a week? He had argued that that was a living wage, but he (Councillor Stott) denied that that was so. He should support the motion without prejudice to his future action in the matter of wages. As long as he remained in the Council he should try to increase the minimum until it became sufficient for a man and his wife and children to live on. They might expect some opposition to the motion because it would mean an increase of the rates, but he could not conceive any man professinc to be reasonable-minded offering such opposition. The increase would only mean about £440 a year. The Corp. ration had no right to be a dumping ground for lazy men—as some members sug- gested it would be—and he hoped that if the heads of any department had men under their control who were not prepared to do a reasonable day's work they would take steps to have them removed and give honest workingmen the opportunity of obtaining employment. Councillor BunToN was sorry to hear Councillor Stott say he was not satisfied with the amount named. It seemed to him (the speaker) that there would he no end of this kind of thing. He was not sure whether a motion of this kind was the proper way in which to bring the matter forward. It would be better for men working for the Corporation who were dissatisfied to apply to the respective committees under which they were employed. (Hear, hear.) If one member after another brought up motions like this over the heads of committees it was no use having committees at all. He was, therefore, sorry he could not see his way to support the proposition, although it was a moderate one. He thought the bulk of the workingmen iii town would prefer that Corporation workmen should be paid at the same rate as employees of private concerns, and he believed if a poll were taken the verdict would be to that effect. Councillor WRIGHT supported the motion,and pointed out that the increased wages would mean a matter of £450 more a year to be spent amongst the poor trades- men who did not know how to keep their heads up. He guaranteed that if they took a poll of the town to-morrow a payment 245. a week would be carried by , an overwhelming majority. No one would vote against it except a few alderman—(langhter)—andione or two I councillors. He had no objection to the advance pro- posed by Councillor Barton; it was something to be go- ing on with. Like Councillor Stott,he was not satisfied with 21s. ; the wage ought to be brought up to a living wage. It was an utter impossibility for a man and his wife and family to live on 215., and it was absurd for Councillor Barton to suggest it. Councillor MAGINN admitted that 21s. was too small a sum for a man to keep a wife and family upon. He suggested that a healthy man who was able-bodied should not take up a child's job but go in for better paid work. But were they all married men who were paid so low ? Were there not some old men amongst the number who had children who, if they were honest, could keep their parents from working ? He was not assuming they should pay any man under 21s. a week who was worth more, but if any man could show him where this kind of motion would stop he would be quite content. If they gave scavengers 21s. the men who were doing better class work would want advancing in pro- portion. It was easy for some to come before the I Council and advertise themselves for the sake of popu- larity, but be thought the proper course would be to , bring the matter before the various committees. Many of the men who were employed by the Corporation could not get work elsewhere owing to age and infirmity. Councillor STOTT : The question of the old men does not come in this resolution. Alderman SMETHURST, who spoke with some warmth, laid he should oppose the motion more particularly in the interests of the men. They had been told that the agitation would not stop at this. As Councillor Magian ' had asked : " Where will all this stop ?" They did not know ; in fact, no one knew. Two or three years ago he said in that Council chamber that it was being converted into a society for the promotion of the wages of the working people, and that was so. (Cries of" No, no.") Never mind it was there. They had a large number of men in the scavenging department wire conld not do any other kind of work than that in which they were , now engaged. How many of them had large families ? I The principal portion were old men and their wives were old women, and was it not better to let them have what they could earn than push them into the workhouse? He had never discharged an old man while he had been in business. He contended that if the resolution were adopted, the places of the old men would be taken by younger ones who were able to do more for the money. Continuing, Alderman Smethurst said he was surprised when he read the Mayor's recent speech. The MAYOR : That is nut on the minutes. The remarks were not put down verbatim. Alderman SMETHURST, continuing, said that if the Council passed the resolution they would deserve all that had been said of them. Councillor AsticitOrr : I should like to reply to the rot Alderman Smethurst spoke. (Laughter). The MAYOR : You have spoken once. Councillor EVANS said he did not know that the resolutionhnd any reference to old men, or that it would interfere with the employment of the Corpora- tion, or that they would be supplanted by younger men. 'rue person at the head of each department, he took it, would be the one to decide who were the able-bodied men and who were not. The proposal of Councillor Barton was he thought a very moderate one, as there were several employers in the town paying more than the amount named. He thought, however, that matters like this should be dealt with by the various committees who he W£lS sn re would deal with them in a reasonable manner. If the employes would make their request to the head of the department end through him to the committee, it would do away with the necessity for motions of this kind and enable the matter to be dealt with more efficiently. He had seen less heat displayed</p>
        <p>when an increase of this amount had been asked for one man than in the presentcase, where 109 men were to be benefited. He thought men on the lowest rung of the ladder who were earning an honest living were entitled to due consideration as much as the men on the top. (Hear, hear). Councillor LORD supported the resolution. He said he would have taken no p trt in the discussion had it not been for the remarks of Alderman Smethurst, who would be the first to get up and propose an increase of salary to some big official. (Laughter.) He contended that these men ought to have some consideration shown to them.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON was pleased with the deep interest Alderman Smethurst ,took in the old men. If able bodied men with families were not to be paid a living wage : he was afraid the future generation would have no old men to take care of. There was no doubt that thousands—he was going to say hundreds of thousands—of children to-day did not get sufficient food to make the necessary bone, sinew and flesh, in very many cases owing to the circumstances of the parents. ! Animals were better looked after in many places. With reference to the suggestion of the men themselves Imaking their requests, be had been on committees where ,1 men had complained and it had been unfortunate for them afterwards—in a certain time the Co,poration had no need for their services. It was only safe for working men to complain through the officials of some organiza- tion they belonged to. It was said that labourers spent ' a great deal of their money in drink,but they knew, also, that drink was now being used less and less, accordi9g to " the trade "itself. The ISlavott : They should brew good beer and/ it would be all right. (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON, continuing, said there was no intention of ousting any old men. Alderman Bs:Ns:Err said he was not seeking popularity in any shape or form and he believed that when a thing was right he ought to support it. He could not help feeling that the resolution was right and therefore he should vote for it. He was not by any means blind to the objections to it—they were quite palpable. One felt that almost every step of social progress was beset with difficulties and we could not extricate ourselves from the gunginore without encountering many of them. The resolution seemed to be one of the first , steps—not a very extravagant one—in the direc- tion of giving something like justice to the , working classes. He thought the Corporation ought to be model employers. They represented the 1 whole community and it was the absolute duty of the whole community to see that those who rendered services to them were properly paid. He was extremely anxious to see the rates reduced„ because he believed that would tend to bring new industries into the town, but there was something higher than. that—one's innate sense of justice—and he could not help thinking that the resolution was one of bare justice. Councillor SKELLAND thought the resolution was a moderate one, and he did not see why any member should oppose it. If a man received a fair day's pay he would do fair day's work. He had seen some ' miserable homes—some of thism the homes of men , working for the Corporation. He did not say they all spent their money in the right way,. but he thought it was impossible for a man to bring up a family properly on less than 21s. a week. (Hear, hear.) Alderman TINNION opposed the resolution. It was not, he contended, the business of the Connell to discuss whether a man could live on 21s. per week. If he could it was a mystery which he (Alderman Tinnion) did not, know how a man solved.. As a matter of fact, however, there were hundreds who had, unfortunately, had to solve the question of how to live on that amount. He objected to the resolution on account of the way in which it was brought,as he thought those matters should come before the various committees employing the men. There had been too much moving by resolution in the Council lately. He objected to the principle of group- ing all the men together, and saying that everyone was worth a guinea a week because he was able-bodied. If there were cases where an able-bodied man was not being paid sufficiently the matter should be brought before the committee, There were many hundreds of men in Warrington not getting 21s, week. (Cries of "Shame,") Yes, it was a shame; but it was true never- theless. They should consider the question apart from all sentiment.because sentiment did not help,A good deal of sympathy had been wasted that night. It was not true and genuine and therefore deserved to be wasted. There were many men who had obtained employment on the Corporation through the influence of members, and he himself had been approached by many men who were willing to " take the brush " owing to baying been out of work so long. There had been some who had sneered at Alderman Sruetburst,but he hoped a lot of men- who had sues red, when they arrived at Alderman Smethurst's age would be able to say they had done as much for the poor and unfortunate as he had done during his 30 years residence in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) They would have more able-bodied labourers, who were not getting so much under private employers, coning to the Corpora- tion for work, and the result would be that the old men now employed would have to go. The resolution, he thought, would do more harm than, good if carried. If they did this they would do a greater injustice than 1 they imagined through displacement by litter and more'l able men. He (Alderman Tinnion) said without hesita- tion that there were men working under some of the ' committees with which be was- connected who, if it were not for the hard times, would not and employment at even 21s. per week. If the wages were increased the services of some would have to be dispensed with. The I lame ratepayers and capitalists had also to be considered. Were the Corporation prepared to introduce such a standard of wages in War- rington that 21s. should be the Lowest for any man ? He suggested that if there were any causes for greivances the matters should be brought before each committee. He felt sure they would do some amount of good in that way without injury to the present employees of the Corporation. Councillor WILSON said that the last time a scheme of that character was brought up by Conncillor Stott he thought it was too ambitious. The present scheme was an extremely moderate one. He thought the shig- gestion, would not, if adopted,restilt in the Corporation getting un overplus of labour as the possibility of over- time at other works was an attraction to men. The MAYOR said that as chairman of the Town Hall, Parks, and Baths, Rivers, Watch and General Purposes Committees be was practically answerable for the carry- ing on of those committees as business concerns. The discussion that night had been practically all senti- mental. No member of the Corporation wished to starve a workingman on 21.5. per week and if he could earn more by putting in extra time no member would stop him. It was only when trade unions came in that they began to hod they would not allow piece-work and that sort of thing and kept them to a set wage. The value of a man's services depended on the labour he could give. As far as the committees of which he was chairman were concerned be nnderstood that there was only one of the employees—a boy—who got less than 21s. a week. He pointed out that when men were engaged at 18s. a week they were commencing on the lowest rung of the ladder. They were not worth more until it was found out what class of work they were suited for. Then, in the Gas Department, for instance, they were promoted to be stokers. He thought the Council should take Alderman Tinnion's advice and send a recommendation to the Chairman of each committee that, as far as possible, '2ls. should be paid to each man who was worth it. He thought the present resolution would amount to advancing one man at the expense of another.</p>
        <p>The motion was then put, and the Afayor declared it carried.</p>
        <p>Councillors STorrand LORD both asked that the names of those voting should be recorded. The MA.voit : Th 3 motion is carried and that is all that is wanted. Councillor LORD said he believed he could claim to have the names recorded under the Municipal Corpora- tions Act. _</p>
        <p>--- Alderman TismoN : I have not the slightest objec- tion.</p>
        <p>Alderman FsinchoMat : I should like my name taken. The way business is conducted in this chamber is something appalling. The llArort: the motion is carried. I don't know what else you want. Does Councillor Barton wish to put somebody in the pillory? Councillor PEMBERTON : He is not asking for it. The MANOR : What is the use of putting the names down?</p>
        <p>Councillor STorr If I can't claim it I aru satisfied. Councillor LORD: T have no doubt about it. I have looked it up. Ask the Deptv,y Town Clerk—he's a qualified man. The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK (the Town Clerk being temporarily absent) : I say take them. The MAYOR : If you want to stand up, you can do so.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON : I shall not stand up again. The names were not taken,</p>
        <p>So far as could be ascertained the voting was : For the resolution : Alderman Bennett, Councillors Pemberton, Lord, Benson, Dickenson, Chantler, Ashcroft, Wilson, Skelland, Barton, Clegg, Evans,Stott, Wright and Brough. Against the resolution : Aldermen Smetharst,Tinnion n d Fairclough, Councillors Wilkinson, Fulton-Smith, snkey, Barton anciHulse.</p>
        <p>IMINIIIIIk</p>
        <p>Councillors ELLISON and Al.to INN did not vote. Councillor BARTON said that with reference to the motion " That each able-bodied man with two years' service or more be given one week's holiday each year" he understood that the men already had holidays and were given a day's wages and would therefore withdraw his resolution. : On the motion of Councillor 13.‘turoN it was decided that the payment of the minimum wage of 21s. come into force as soon as practicable after January Ist ; 1900.</p>
      </div>
