      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the meeting of the ! above on Sunday. Air. C. H. Mills presided and spoke upon" Good done and good received." Mr. F.J. Adock of Manchester was the speaker, his subject being " Religious difficulties." Taking Zaacheus in the scyamore tree, his small statue, his calling as a tax- i gathererMe climbed into the tree to see .lesuis and to be t unseen by the crowd. In that he failed, Jesus and the people saw him and Christ went to the house of , Zaccheus. Christ wants our home, livens and lives. !We should be willing to give up all to him. Mr. A. Healey and the Secretary also took pArt, in the meet- ing and Mr. W. H. Payton, Mits. Bac.,presided at the , organ.</p>
        <p>"SIR CHRISTOPHER FURNESS, MT. AND HIS SUCCESSFUL STEE L AND COAL. CO, 1 FAMES." The advantages anticipated from the triple alliance of the three companies registered under the names of the Weardale Steel, Coal, and Coke; the Cargo Fleet ; and the South Durham Steel and Iron, have been amply demonstrated by the success which has attended the union during the few months it has been in operation. The profits of all three concerns have been increased, under the guidance of their able chairman, Sir Christopher Furness, M. I-'. A comparison of the accounts is interesting reading. In the case of the South Durham Steel the profit for the nine months was 1109,123 against £80,095 for the previous twelve months. The Weardale Steel accounts cover a period of fifteen months, and the gross profits are £101,766, as against £75,186 the previous year. The usual G per cent, dividend has been declared, mid the carry-forward •Is. .f.:34,220, against. £24,805. The profits from the Cargo Fleet allow of a 5 per cent, distribution, and th, notwithstanding the fact that the corks were not completed at the end of the financial year. On a close study of the affairs of the allied companies it is safe to assume that the minimum distribution to the Ordinary shareholders will be 7!,, per cent. if matters remain as they are, but. the concerns are yet young under the new organisation, and the possibilities in front of them are great with the present able management, this making the shares of the , companies a promising investment,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL. At the meeting of this Council on Wednesday, the medical °nicer (Dr. M•Dougall) reported that during November there wore 21 deaths and 43 birth registered. compared with 26 deaths and 43 births registered in the corresponding month last year. The council decided to support the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority in their application to the Local Government Board to be invested with powers to seize unsound articles intended for the food or man. The Local Government Board sanctioned the horrgaing of .2130 for fifteen years for the retaining all wir South Bank-terrace, which has been declared a higimay.</p>
