      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MY DEAR CHILDREI</head>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREI</p>
        <p>Further down in this column you will see an essay on Dartford, sent to us by Ethel Manders, as this is so very good I have sent her an extra prize in addition to the one awarded last iveek. Reginald Sharman has sent me a story about a tiger which I publish this .week. I wish more of you would send me such tales, and I shall be pleased to print the best in the Corner, Christmas will be drawing very, near by the time you get this paper' I do hope that many of you have done your best to send in, contributions for the Christmas CoMpetitions. They are all easy, and you have a variety of subjects, so I hope I shall receive a great many, and I shall have very 'great pleasure in awarding the nice prizes that I promised. . .</p>
        <p>In the future I shall want you to all send me poetry with about four to six verses, also short stories, you can either invent these or take them from some of your books. I want the Children's Corner to become, by degrees, a contribution by its members. Of course I shall award prizes for the pieces sent me. lam sure you all like to see what you have written in print. With all good wishes from UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>ESSAY ON "TILE PLACE I LIVE IN-DARTFORD,"</head>
        <p>ESSAY ON "TILE PLACE I LIVE IN-DARTFORD,"</p>
        <p>Dartford is situated in the valley of the river DRrenth, with a hill rising abruptly on either side, over which the town has straggled for some dis- tance. The main street is called High Street, and in it are all the best shops. The town is a curious mixture of the ancient and the modern. In High Street is the house in which the famous VVat Tyler once resided • also an Inn with a Court-yard into which the old Stage Coaches used to drive and wait while the passen- gers alighted and refreshed themselves. At one end of the same street stands a fine old church with a solid square tower, and thick strong walls. At the top of one of the hills which is called East Hill is the site of the burning of many mar- tyrs whose lives were taken during the Jeligious persecutions. About half-way up the same hill is an old burial ground in which a memorial has been erected to their Memory. There are two recreation grounds, one at the top of East Hill and the other in the centre of the town.</p>
        <p>There are several factories, although Dartford is a small town. One of them, that of Burroughs, Wellcome &amp; Co., manufacturing chemists, is nearly covered with greenery, which gives the, building a nice appearance. Dartford is surrounded by lovely country, some of which is marred by public institutions, such as lunatic asylums. . . . _ . _</p>
        <p>During -the day the town is quiet, but at night when it is lit up and the people are liberated from their various employments it presents a very animated appearance. EDITH MANDERS (Aged 15).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>UNCLE GEORGE'S TIGER STORY.</head>
        <p>UNCLE GEORGE'S TIGER STORY.</p>
        <p>"Now, Uncle George," said Milly one evening after tea, "We are all ready to hear - a story about wild animals." As Uncle George had been in India, he at once said, "Sit down, then, and I will tell you a story about a tiger." "Is it a true story ?" asked Fred, "and did you know any of the people who took part in it?" "Oh yes, the story is true, every word of it," replied Uncle George, "and I knew three or four of the persons who had most to do with it. And now for the story. A lady and a gentleman with their baby, a little, boy, were once travelling through a lonely part of India. One night, they had to sleep, in the open. In the early hours of the morning the lady started up and cried out, 'Oh, my baby, my baby. Where is my baby?' Her husband heard her cry, and looking into the cot he saw that the baby was not there. In great alarm he ran out of the tent, followed by his wife, and saw by the light of the moon a great animal moving slowly off towards the wood with something white in its mouth. They at once woke the servants, and takin2 their loaded guns, they set out in pursuit. They hurried as fast as they could, yet making very little noise for fear the animal, which was a tiger, would hear them and run far away into the forest. Soon they saw through the trees that the tiger was lying down and was playing with the baby just as a cat plays with a mouse before she kills it. Oh how sad the poor mother felt. How she cried to the men to save her child. What could the father do? He was afraid that it he took aim at the tiger he might miss the animal and hit his child. Just then one of the men raised his gun to shoot the tiger. The laay, seeing him, cried out, '0 you will kill my child, you will kill my child.' But the man fired with a true aim, and the tiger, jumping up, gave a loud cry and fell down dead. Then they all ran forward, and there was the baby quite gale and smiling, as if he were not at all frightened. It was a good thing for the little fellow that he had no idea of the danger he had been in." "Oh, uncle, what a strange story. And did the baby really live?" "Yes, the lady was ill from fright, but the baby was not hurt at all. I have often seen him since then." "Oh, have you really seen the boy who has been in a tiger's mouth ? ' "Yes, I have and</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LETTER CAKE.</head>
        <p>THE LETTER CAKE.</p>
        <p>(Concluded). "With me! 7 I cried. "Why, I thought she was in the kitchen, blowing bubbles."</p>
        <p>" Pebbles ?" says Betty.— "There's no pebbles in the house, ma'am—nothing but fine white sand." It seemed not a word about bubbles had ever reached Betty's ears. She had been busy every minute, and had not thought of the child. Dinner was ready, now; but I wauld not sit down till we had found Lena.</p>
        <p>"She must be upstairs," I said. Betty thought not. 'Don't you remember I was in your eftatnber, ma'am, half an hour ago, to get you a .spool of silk out of your ivory box, and wouldn't I have seen her, if she had been there?"</p>
        <p>"Never mind, Betty. You go again and I'll go with you." We went from room to rcort up- stairs, calling " Lena!" but no answer came. Then we searched the attic in every corner, then the cellar—no Lena was to be found. She could not have left the house, for we kept every door locked and bolted. She could not have gone out, unless somebody from outside had picked a lock and come in and stolen her! Tliat wasn't at all likely. Somebody might have done it while poor, deat Betty was down cellar getting potatoes: I knew this was not so; still—where was the child? We hunted the house over and over till I was ready to drop ; and then I had to send for Mrs. Paul, and-ask what was to be done. She came in, quite.out of breath and sadly frightened, with a policeman close at her ,heels. The policeman insisted on searching the house again. This would make the sixth time; but Betty Said not a word, nor did I; we merely followed him. " I su i ppose you've looked n all the closets ? " said he.</p>
        <p>"In every one but mine," I answered; "that is always locked, and she can't have got in there; but here's the key, if you like—here in my pocket." He took the key, opened the door—and there, if you'll believe it, was that missing baby curled up on a shelf, sound asleep ! She must have slipped in when Betty went up after the silk, and Betty had locked the door upon her without knowing it. You may fancy how the child was hugged and kissed, and how her mother cried over her.</p>
        <p>"1 spealced to Betty two times," said Lena; "but she didn't let me out, and didn't let me Out."</p>
        <p>"After dinner, when everybody was gone, and I had taken my nap, Betty came into the parlor, and I knew by the way she cleared her throat that she had something to say.. "There's new coal on in the range, ma'am, and if you don't object, where's the harm in just making a Christmas cake for the baby, seeing as I shut her up, and scared folks so?" "Not the least harm, Betty. Only be sure you stuff it as full as it will hold with raisins and citron and currants and everything nice." Betty laughed at that. I knew the cake would be a wonder, and so it was. The very odor of it put me in high spirits at once,</p>
        <p>"And now, ma am, I'm thinking," added Betty, clearing her throat again, "would it do to frost it?"</p>
        <p>"Frost it as white as the driven snow, Betty. And trace her name on the top with little red candy drops." Betty was in raptures; but I might have known - she couln't spell. When she brought the cake to me with great pride, I ran my fingers over the name, and found it Was L-E-A-N-E-R.</p>
        <p>" Beautiful," said I, and didn't tell her there were too many letters in it. I dare say she thought the darling deserved them all and a dozen more. Lena was overjoyed with the cake. It outshone for her the costliest gifts on the Christmas • tree, they said. Dear baby! That night she added to her prayers another "bless- ing," which warmed Betty Fay's old heart through and through: " Please bless Betty—can't hear—made me a boo-1111 Kismas fwosted letter-cake!"</p>
