      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WARRINGTON GYMNASIUM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>Annual Display and Wrestling ;1 Cumpetition.</p>
        <p>Evidence of the interest which is being taken in the work of the %Val rington Borough Gymnasium was afforded by the large attendance at the thirteenth annual gymnastic, display and wrestling competition which were held on Monday evening. There can be no two opinions as to the valuable purpose which the institution serves in imparting sound physical training and it is pleasing to know that the excellent facilities are largely taken advantage of. Some of the most interesting features of Monday's display were provided by members of the gymnastic classes who demonstrated their skill with the dumb-bells,bar-bellsaud indian clubs, and gave clever performances on the rings,horizontal har,parallel bars, and vaulting horse. Special praise is due to. Mr. A. Alottram the instructor and Mr. A. Kinsley who directed the exercises. The great attraction of the evening was a wrestling competition for which there were eight entries. Some splendid bouts were witnessed in the first round, the results being :—D. C. Williamson beat J. Young (Liverpool), time 1 min. 18 Sec.; T. Hallwood (Liverpool), beat T. James (Liverpool), time 5 min. 50 see; J. Tinkles (Warrington), beat S. Price, (Warrington), on points, the time being 10 minutes without a fall ; G. Topping (Warrington), beat J. Taylor (Warrington), time 1 min. 25 secs. In the second round Hallvvood beat Topping after a struggle lasting 4 mins. and 10 secs. Williamson and Tinkles wrestled for ten minutes without a fall being obtained and Tinkles was awarded the victory on points. The , final produced it fine contest, Hallwoodl who is a noted wrestler and the winner of the Gordon Athletic Clubs Championship for 1001, having a doughty opponent in Tinkles who w: s encouragedby the cheers of the Warring ton enthusiasts. Hallwood gained the first fall in 4 mins. and 10 secs. but Tinkler, amid considerable enthusiasm, proved the victor in the second bout which lasted only a minute and a half. The deziiing bout, however, was won after a tough struggle of 4 min. 30 secs. by Hall- wood.</p>
        <p>The gathering was presided over by Mr. HENRY 'ROBERTS, J.P., who in the Course of a short address said much was being done at present ia regard to education, hut while we were doing some very substantial and very necessary things in matters of elementary education he thought we hardly realized that in regard to secondary education—which, to his mind, was infinitely more important—we had hardly yet touched the fringe of what was necessary if the next generation were to take their proper place, fully equipped, mentally and other- wise, in competition for the world's trade. (Hear, hear.) If it was necessary to pursue this work so far as the mind was concerned, it was necessary to give the mind something substantial to rest upon in the shape of a strong and healthy body, and that was where the value of an institution like the gymnasium came in (Hear. hear.) Those upon whom our political and commercial future rested were being well provided for, and if they I they only made use of the opportunities afforded them he had no fear that the generation to follow those who had for some time taken an active part in the affairs of the town would fully justify all the money that had been spent for their benefit. He had pleasure in recog- nizing the good work which this institution had done, and was still doing, especially with regard to the chil- dren attending the elementary schools. (Applause). At the oonclusion of the contest Alderman T 1 NNION moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman and said it was largely through Mr Roberts's tact and persuasive eloquence that the recent difficulty with regard to the physical instruction of elementary school children wes removed, and a scheme which he (Alderman Tinnion)\ considered would be of great advantage to the children Was adopted to the mutual satisfaction of both committees. I (Applause.) , Councillor WRIGHT seconded, and the vote was heartily accorded. I In responding, Mr. ROBERTS said that although for ' some time past he had not taken an active part in town's affairs, everything which conduced to the moral, material and social interests of the inhabitants, was very near and dear to his heart. He hoped be would still have many other opportunities of serving his fellow ' townsmen. (Applause). • IMiss A. Clayton presided at the piano, and ably accompanied the various exercises.</p>
      </div>
