      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Unhealthy Parr-street.</head>
        <p>Unhealthy Parr-street.</p>
        <p>S.D." writes to complain of the semi-flooded and unhealthy state of Parr-street, and says that the street was scheduled to be paved three or four years ago. He receives frequent complaints from residents and appeals to use his influence with the representatives of the Ward to get the street attended to. He adds : "I know from experience that the Chairman and Com- mittee are anxious to get on with the paving of all the streets, therefore I beg on behalf of the people living there that they will at once rectify this long-standing grievance."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Industrial Betterment at Messrs. ' Crosfield's Works.</head>
        <p>Industrial Betterment at Messrs. ' Crosfield's Works.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l was surprised to see under this heading in your last issue the full text of the American Institute's weekly commercial letter with its lavish compliment to myself personally. No doubt it was sent to you with the best of intentions by some well-wisher of the works, and I take this opportunity of thanking him for the service. At the same time I take leave to say that had the letter been first submitted to me, I could not have consented to its insertion in full. Apart from suppressing the personal element I should certainly have corrected the candle story. For the honour of the Crostletd family let me hasten to say that Mrs. Cranston's version is a travesty of the facts. The Mr. Crosfleld named as the uncle of the present Messrs. Crostleld though not the founder of the firm was the author of the candle idea. So far the story is authentic but in every other respect it is apocryphal. The truth is that in those days, soap being like a thonsand other com- modities subject to a tax, its manufacture laboured under irritating Excise restrictions. One of these demanded the presence of the Excise officer so long as there was a fire under a pan. By the simple device then of employing a man all night to hold a candle under the soap-pan, the officer was kept in attendance until the workpeople arrived in the early morning when work could be started without delay. It will be seen that the stratagem was directed not against the workpeople but against the common Enemy the revenue man, and against him as we know in those dark days of Protection any trick was fair.—l am, &amp;c., K. E. MARKEL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Cup and Medals Presented.</p>
        <p>--- The presentation of the cup and medals won in con- nection with the Warrington Draughts League took place in the Cairo-street Schools on Thursday evening. There was a large attendance of members of the League and the evening was spent in playing a side match, almost the whole of those present taking part. Mr.R.A. Naylor should have distributed the prizes but he being unable to attend this duty was performed by the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, who in doing so expressed his delight at the deep interest which was taken in what was a game of skill. Draughts,being a'game of strategy,called into play some of the higher faculties,and therefore to his mind a good draught player, unless he WAS a gambler at the game could not be an immoral man. Draughts required a concentration of thought and perhaps the greatest mischief of the present age was that there was a lack of concentration. (Applause.) Mr. Cook then handed the silver cup and gold centre medals to the Monks Hall team, winners of the first division of the League. and gold centre medals to the Latchford Baptist team winners of the second division.—ln responding to a vote of thanks which was heartily accorded to him Mr. Cook said he was one of those parsons who thought there was not enough play. What he meant was that at the present time 30,000 people watched 30 play. He thought that if 30 people watched 30,000 play the country would be richer in health and physique. (Hear, hear.) During an interval refreshments were served after which play was resumed, a number of exciting contests being witnessed, and a very enjoyable ',evening being spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION FIXTURES.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>For Agst. Sept. 2 Leigh home 7 0 9 Oldham home 2 0</p>
        <p>903.</p>
        <p>, 16 Broughton „ 23 Wigan away 3 11 , 30 Halifax avay 10 10 Oct. 7 Barrow home 13 0 „ 9 Hull Kingston R home 5 8 „ 14 Rochdate (cup-tie) away 10 0 „ 16 Bradford „ 21 Oldham away 0 8 „ 28 Leigh (cup-tie) home , 2 5 Noy. 4 Runcorn away 2 5 „ 11 Rochdale „ 18 Hunslet . „ 25 Swinton away 3 12 Dec. 2 Widnes „ 9 Runcorn „ 15 Dewsbury away ~ 23 St.lielens home „ 25 Leigh away „ 25 Salford borne , 30 Broughton away 1905 Jan. 1 Dewsbury „ 6 Leeds home „ 13 Hull ~ 20 Swinton , 27 Salford away Feb. 3 Millom home „ 10 Miliom away „ 17 „ 21 Barrow „ 27 Bradford Mar. 3 N.U.Cup Ist Round „ 10 Wigan „ 17 Leeds away ~ 24 WidneS „ 31 Rochdale Hornets away Apl. 7 Hull „ 13 Halifax (G.P) home 14 1-funslet home ,„ 16 St. Helens away</p>
        <p>The Conservative majority in the New Forest Division has fallen from 755 to 199, and the Liberal poll has increased from 3,726 to 4,340, and yet our Conservative friends look upon the election as a victory for them. How have the mighty fallen !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 9, 1905.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOWLING LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL DINNER AND PRESENTATION</p>
        <p>OF CUP AND MEDALS. ---- Mr. A. H. Crosfield and the Value of</p>
        <p>Games.</p>
        <p>Although only in the second year of its existence, the Warrington and District Bowling League is already one of the most popular of the sporting organizations of Warrington and a successful future may safely be predicted for it. Last season's competi- tion was entered into with great keenness and after a series of splendid games the Bull's Head team proved the winners of the silver championship cup presented by Mr. A. H. Crosfield and the gold medals. 'There was a very pleasant function at the Lion Hotel on Thursday evening, when the annual dinner in connec- nection with the League was held and Mr. A. H. Cros- field,the popular president,presented the cup,medals,and prizes. The large muster of bowlers testified to the Interest which was taken in the occasion. Mr. Oros- field presided, and was supported by Alderman Evans (president of the Bull's head team), Mr. E. England, Mr. W. J. Shaw, Mr. J. Rushworth, Mr. E. Taylor (secretary), and others. An excellent repast was served by Mr. Lee, and afterwards a " smoker " was held.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, in proposing the health of " The King" said it was a toast which all Englishmemwhether sportsmen or not—and he was glad to know that a great many Englishmen were sportsmen—always received with unaffected and most sincere loyalty—a loyalty that only increased as year by year they saw how much they and the country weie indebted to the energy, the tact, the hard-working ability, and the delightful urbanity of his Majesty. (Applause.) ! The toast was drunk with musical honours. Alderman E VANS briefly proposed the toast of "Our President," observing that if he spoke for half-an-hour he did not think he could tell them more than they knew of the sportsmanlike conduci of their president. He might safely say that the Warrington League would have had no existence but for Mr. Crosfleld's assistance. He started the League in a sportsmanlike manner and had continued to support it ever since both by his purse and countenance. He would ask the meeting to drink to the health of " the finest sportsman that they had turned out in Warrington." (Applause). The toast was drunk with great cordiality, the company rising and singing " For he's a jolly good fellow."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PRIZES.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZES.</p>
        <p>Mr. CaosriaLD then presented the prizes. The winners of the cup and gold medals were the Bull's Head team, which is composed as follows :—R. Fairbrother (captain), A. Shaw. A. Gleave, John Bird, John Swanton, J. W. Jolley, E. Shaw, H. Clare, J. W. Worsley, W. Simister, F. Berry. The silver medals were won by the Sankey Chapel House team, the following being the members :—J. Goulden (captain), W. Rutter, T. Forster, H. Houghton, W. Ellison, S. Fletcher, J. Jackson, A. Warburton, J. Barton, Joseph Unsivorth, J. Spark, J. De-akin. The winner of the prize for the best aggregate in the League Competition was A.Shaw(Bull's Head) Who was victorious in every match at home and away. The award consisted of a certificate of merit and order on a tradesman.. Mr. England's prize, value 5, which was competed for on the new green at Victoria Park, was won as follows :-1, Mr. Thomas Ellison, cheque; Mr. H. Clare, cheque. Mr. CROSELELD heartily con gni tulated Mr. Fairbrother on the fine victory the Bull's Howl team had achieved. He hoped they would be victorious in many more contests in Warrington. (Applause.) Mr. FAIIIIIROTIIErt, in respoading, said they felt very proud to have Mr. Crosfield as president of the League. They hoped he would hold the office for many years and that the League would continue to prosper. (Applause.) Addressing Mr. Ellison, Mr. Crostield said that although he had never taken a foremost place in bowls, still as a very old howler—having played the game longer than he had played any other outdoor sport—it gave him honest satisfaction to have the pleasure of presenting the cheque to the champion bowler of War- rington. (Applause.) Mil. CROSFIELD AND THE VALUE OF' GAMES. Mr. CROSFIELD then delivered a short address in response to the toast of "Our President." He expressed regret that be would not be able to participate in the pleasant social intercourse of that gathering for long as it was necessary for him to keep an appointment with a gentleman—not to talk about bowls or gslf but of something much more serious. Continuing, he said that last year he was very proud indeed that a bowling club NN ith which he had beeu in close touch and sympathy for many years won thechampionahip of the Warrington Bowling League. But although he had bad to speak on many occasions when bowling victories had been achieved—and incidentally he might say when golfing triumphs had been celebrated—be had never risen to congratulate winners more heartily and sincerely than ho desired to congratulate the Bull's Head team on their performance. (Applause.) Above all he was pleased to congratulate his old political opponent and he was sure be might say his very good friend Alderman Evans. (Hear, hear). The alderman had said something kind about him ass sports- man but he (Mr. Crosfield) could give their friend a little tu vogue because Alderman Evans was one of the best sportsmen they had in Warrington. In him the Bull's Head club had a president who was universally popular, and one with whom it had been a great pleasure to him (Mr. Crosfield) to come in contact on similar occasions for years past. He trusted they might look forward to meeting Alderman Evans on those sporting and pleasant occasions for many a year to come. (Applause). In that room the other day he had the privilege of speaking in the presence of a great number of brother Foresters and he heard—not for the first time—that as a Forester he was' entitled to claim a pretty long descent—a descent that went back—they were very modest—as far as Adam. (Laughter.) That night he had been wondering how far they as bowlers could claim to go back. We in England were proud , of the beautiful old country seats—one of the greatest charms of the land—which so many of our public- , spirited noblemen threw open to the public. One of the features of those ancient seats %V IS the quaint 1 bowling greens. It struck him, hovvever,that the game I must have a much longer history than that, because bowls were so popular in Warrington that he could not I help thinking there must have been some bowler in those ancient days when the town was founded. (Laugh- ter.) They had a little discussion among the Foresters as to how far back Warrington went and he believed that the origin of Warrington like the origin of bowls was probably lost in the mist of ages. (Laughter.) He was afraid golfers could not lay claim to a history like that, although they had got a long way back at St. Andrews. The Scotchtuen thought they invented the game in the days of King Charles, or some time before that—(laughter)—but they found that the game was played on ice in Holland before it went to Scotland. The other day when he had the luck in competition with some , very good English and American players to pull off that ' championship near Paris—(applause) —he was shown, to his amazement, a picture of golfers long before the Scotch and the Dutch, and he began to think golf must he nearly as old again as howls. (Laughter.) It seemed that in the fourteenth century the monks who wrote in manuscript the beauti- fully illustrated books of that age used to ornament them with little pictures of the customs of the day and the games that were played, and so their French friends had produced one of those games and called it golf. (Laughter.) He had seen the picture of that style of golf but be hardly thought his friend Alderman Evans —who was addicted to the game—would choose one of the clubs shown in that illustration. (Laughter.) He did did not think either that if bowlers had the chance of going hack to pre-historic times, they would have any particular bias in favour of the bowls said to be used at that period. (Laughter.) Bowls and golf,like all other games, had made progress and he hoped they would continue to do so. He did not want to see the fighting instinct die out of Englishmen—(applause)--neither did he believe in any Utopia of peace that was going to be reached in a moment or two. He was not, however) going to touch that point. What he would emphasise was that while they wanted to see the fighting instinct preserved, they also wished to see a great deal of it directed into good healthy channels. It was often said that games ran away with us in England. We must keep our sense of proportion and use games as a means to an end, and remember our duties as public citizens quite apart from the field of sport, not forgetting that the constant, healthy rivalry that we had in England in various fields of sport not only helped us as, the old Duke said, to win Waterloo but had also helped to mould English character, and to develop that fine, fair sense of comradeship which taught us never to hit below the belt. (Applause). It was a very great mistake for people to begin running down, in any shape or form, the character of Englishmen. We had a character to ,be proud of—not to boast of, but to live up to. He submitted in all confidence that our outdoor games and contests helped Englishmen very much to cultivate the I genuine, open-hearted character of which they need not be ashamed wherever they might travel throughout the , wide world. (Applause). "THE LEAGUE" AND OTHER TOASTS. Mr. E. ENGLAND proposed the toast of "The War- rington and District Bowling League." He and many others had watched the doings of the organization with considerable interest, and they were not at all surprised at the success which it had attained, especially when they considered what an excellent President it had, and also how much it owed to the efforts of Alderman Evans, who was one of the best sportsmen in the town, and to the worthy Secretary. (Applause.) Mr.W.J.Stissw responded and said the hoisours which workingmen had gained in the League had been a source of great gratification. He hoped that local bowlers would be more united in the future and thatinext year</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ENGLAND'S DOWNFALL</head>
        <p>ENGLAND'S DOWNFALL</p>
        <p>ON THE SLOPES OF SYDENHAM. New Zealanders Still Unbeaten.</p>
        <p>A COMPARISON OF METHODS.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>lam sorry for England —in a football sense ; and Protection is badly needed also in a football sense. Here again the political argument of the Free Traders holds good, for the kind of Protection that is most required is a perfection of methods and general up-to-dateness. The competition of the New Zealanders fox the supply of good football need not be feared,and no tariff is 'necessary to keep then out. They are, rather, to be welcomed, as they have been welcomed all over England, Scotland and Ireland.. They are a fine lot of fellows, and their visit will do English footballers a deal of good. Still I must repeat that I am sorry for poor old England. She is in a hipeless case, so far as her international status in Rugby football is concerned. .</p>
        <p>My readers will, doubtless, have read innumerable reports of the Crystal Palace match, and be fully conversant with the solemn facts that the English. team never scored a point, or raised a hope of success in the breasts of the sixty or seventy thousand people gathered together on the slopes of Sydenham. I shall . endeavour, therefore, to touch upon matters which' escape the attention of the hurrying " stop press "1 journalist and make some comparisons which may be interesting to Northern Unionism The Crystal Palace ground, in the first place, is admirably adapted for gladiatorial contests of this nature. It is a natural amphitheatre, open only at one end, and on three sides banked to such a height as to afford ample accommodation for a crowd of 100,000 people. With judicious erpenditure it could be greatly improved from the spectators' point of view, but it is not yet given over entirely to• football. Nevertheless the onlooker has little to complain of at the Palace. He can see the match, as thousands did, for next to nothing. It was a decidedshock, seeing how one has to pay for things in London, to find on paying the is. adriaission to the Palace buildings and grounds that this small sum covered the match as well. The whole of the south and east sides of the ground and embankment were free, while the remainder of the accommodation consisted of stands and seats which were, of course, well paid for. At one time the playing pitch was a lake-bed, and on Saturday it almost suggested as much at a glance, there was so much moisture about. Still the grass was fairly luxuriant, and the only complaint could be on account of the softness of the soil underneath. It was certainly very " heavy " in a football sense, and made speedy passing and running almost impossible. But, I anticipate. To come to the match, let me say right hero that if the display of the Englishmen was a fair sample of the best football under the Rugby Union, then it is indeed in a parlous state. There are a dozen Northern Union Club teams in Lancashire and Yorkshire which would have upheld the honour of Old England against the `• All Blacks " on Saturday in a far more credit- able style than did the chose&amp; fifteen. Warrington's cup team of last season, for instance, would have made a more respectable showing and would not, in I my opinion, have been beaten pointless. The English- I men were hopeless. For such forward deficiency as there was there is some excuse in the more perfect . and scientific methods of the visitors, but the three- ' quarter line was appalling in its ineptitude. The backs appeared to have no idea of passing and running, or combination one with another. Even the "All Blaeks " had failed to teach them the rudiments of the game. I am not exaggerating when I say that on no single occasion in the whole game did the ball travel from the English halves to the wings by way of a passing movement. And they had the ball often enough to make some pretence to attack, if they had had the capacity in them. But they .appeared devoid of all cleverness or ability as Rugby footballers either individually or combined. We have heard and read a lot about Braithwaite's worrying tactics, Hind's pace,Shewring's solidity and soundness in the centre, and Raphael's individual brilliance. Where were they on Saturday? Absolutely non- existent. Not a man in the English attacking line displayed enough enterprise or dash to save him from being at once given marching orders by the manage- ment of any senior Northern Union Club. The whole division were a ghastly . and complete failure. It may be urged that they set themselves out to play a defensive game. If they did, it was rank bad policy- and not well carried out at that. The best defence is attack and there was nothing on Satut day to prevent a smart team from doing a considerable amount of attacking against the New Zealanders.The defence,as such,left a lot to be desired. The men of the red rose were fre- quently caught out of place, and Hind was a great sinner in this respect or he would not have had four tries scored on his wing. Our New Zealand friends are nothing if not knowing, and they evidently weT well aware of the softest place in the English armour for they pegged away at it all afternoon. Then the tackling of the home backs was far from being effec- tive. They pluckily went for the man but they were generally too late to prevent him passing the ball. Half an hour of the quick Northern Union spotting and robust low tackling would have made an immense difference to the fortunes of the English side. At back, Jackett was safe, but distinctly moderate in other respects. He would have had no claims to County hanours in the Northern Union. I can now leave the Englishmen to stew in the bitterness of their ova reflections and turn to the all conquering " All-Blacks." I Frankly, I was a bit disappointed. The conditions told against the wearers of the silver fern giving their best display, but to one accustomed to the " devil " and dash of the encounters up here their methods seemed just a little slow. But they were wonderfully sure, and fall of variety. I have seen picker handling and speedier running on worse turf than that at the Palace on Saturday by Northern Union backs, but I never saw such persistent and resourceful passing. It is pass, pass, pass, all the time with the -New Zealanders and the forwards seem almost as good at the game as the rear-men. It was very noticeable that when the" All Black" forwards did do any rushing they never attempted to dribble the ball to the line, or fall on it to save. At the first opportunity one of them would pick up with unerring certainity and sling the ball out to one of the hacks hovering near, or to a fellow-forlyard. The result was the same in either case. A passing run was inaugurated, and there was danger in the air. Then, too, the " All Blacks " do very little touch-kicking or punting at all. They do not work their way up the field by touch-play from the -halves—this is an anti- quated method with them. They pass and re-pass until there is room far a run, and when the man with ' the ball does get away he is always backed up in wonderful fashion by at least a couple of colleagues. I Once on the move, and they are a very difficult emai- -1 bination to check. The system of the conquerors has only one secret—brains. They have thought out every movement in the game, and they turn their complete understanding to such account that the very moderate defence of the English teams is easily baffled. To take an instance. I was in a good position to see how easily the first try was obtained. Gallaher, that fine "spoiler " and leader was on the side of a scrum near I the line. The Ball came out to him instead of to the I half-back Roberts —perhaps intentionally—and he at once made a feint for the line. Hind and everybody else near enough made a rush for the All-Black skipper and left the wing unguarded. Naturally the wily Gallaher just handed the oval to . McGt egor who touched dawn without needing any I sprinting abilities. 1 venture to say that a crack ' Northern Union team would not have been "done " so simply, but that by the way: McGregor got three ' , more tries on the same wing as the outcome of I accurate passing which left hint with an easy journey. The Englishmen were too late in their efforts to , check the machine-like movements of their opponents. Against a Northern Union side the attack of the New ZealandeLs would often have been smothered in its infancy, but they would still out-point any team in- the Union. Why? The men are not superior ; we ' have as good if not better individual stars than the Maorilanders. This is where the original formation of the New Zealand side comes in. Judging as well as possible from the poor back play opposed to it, I should say that the one lialf-back,two five-eighths,and three-threequarter game . is a better system than the universal four threeqttarter game played in England. It affords more opportunities ' for combination and for developing an attack on either I side unexpectedly. The half back has two five-eighths behind him and a little to the left an right. When he gets the ball he runs just so far as to draw the defence towards him. Then he passes—to the one</p>
        <p>who has the best chance of developing a successful I passing run. A. brainy half can see in a moment where to send the ball and Roberts is just the man for the post. In the English game the half backs run either left or right round the " scrum " and 1 the play at once swings over to that side. Not so with the New Zealanders. Until j'the hall-back has actually parted with the ball there is no means of the other side knowing which way the attack is likely to go. The ball may go one way to a five-eighth and he smartly swung back the opposite direction to the dismay of the defending side. There is no doubt, in my mind, that our clever cousins from the Antipodes have solved the problem of always turn- . 'mg possession of the ball into a certain means of offence. Lined out the men appear to fit their positions with the symmetry of a diagram, and the English back division looked decidedly ragged in comparison. But on Saturday that division appeared to have no know- ledge of its proper place. The success of the " All Blacks' "methods reads a leelson to Northern Unionists as well as English Rugby Unionists. Already the " amateur" teams are adopting the visitors' formation, or some approach to it, but a lot of practice will be required to bring about perfection. Perhaps the sternest lesson which is being taught us is that brainy combination and clever passing can °Jerome any amount of dash, avoirdupois, and i punting ability. The Northern Union has, perhaps, raised the passing game to the pinnacle of excellence , under the four-threequarter rules, and those teams which have cultivated handling most have been the most popular and successful. Witness, Broughton Rangers. Their half and three-quarter combination iis superior to anything else in the Union and the consequence is that they pat up the biggest perform- , ances and draw the largest gates. Warrington's halcyon days were when the backs were running and passing with speed and precision, even though there was less muscle and weight in the team. It is the passing game that wins matches and rejoices the heart of the spectator ; and the " All Blacks" have perfected the best method yet extant. That method will have to be seriously considered by the Northern Union legislators. They have got the men, and if they can introduce a method which will combine efficiency and attractiveness as does that of the New Zealanders then the future of the game is secure. I have always maintained that Rugby at its best is superior to Association, and Rugby as the New Zealanders play it, is not only at its best, but it is the finest ball game either under the sun, or the leaden winter skies of Old England, All hail ta the " All Blacks !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LOCAL RECHABITISM.</head>
        <p>LOCAL RECHABITISM.</p>
        <p>HIGH CHIEF RULER'S VISIT TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>State Prohibition Advocated.</p>
        <p>The workers of the Warrington District of the Independent Order of Rechabites had the pleasure of a visit from the High Chief Ruler of the Order, Bro. C. W. Churchill, on Saturday. An informal conference was held in the Rigby Memorial Hale:Wycliffe Church, in the afternoon and was largely attended. In the unavoidable absence of the Rev. John Vonge, the meet- ing was presided over by Bro. R. Harrison, D.C.R. (Garswood), who was 'supported by Bro. B. Walker, P.D.C.R.' Bro. S. Fernley (Secretary of the Union Tent) and Bro. W. Wright, P.D.S.J.T. In addition to the members of the Warrington District, delegates were present from Southport, Manchester, Stockport, Heywood and Runcorn. Mr. FERNLEY announced the receipt of apologies for non-attendance from the Rev. John Yonge, Mr. F. H. Newton, Councillor Brough, Mr. Josiah Longland, and Mr. E. V. Walker. The Hiatt CHIEF RULER said that Lancashire was looked upon as the very Mecca of Rechabitism. If the movement was not flourishing in Lancashire they could not expect it to be doing so in any part of the country. The great work which they had to do to-day was not so much to push on with gigantic strides the big Districts of the Order which were strong enough to carry on their own extension work, as to advance the positioa of weaker and smaller districts. It had been a dream of his that, except in one or two instances where it was physically impossible for them to do so, they should have no District in the Order In a very few years time that had not at least 500 members. Only on Friday he got the Board of Directors to help in the extension scheme particularly, and every member of the Board had given into his charge certain smaller districts of the Order which he had to look after, encourage, and help in every way he possibly could. There ought not to be a town or village having one thousand inhabitants where the people had not the opportunity of joining the Order. Dealing with the progress of the Order in Lancashire, Bro. Churchill said that fifteen years ego there were in the county 12,669 members of the Order, whereas at the close of 1901 there were 19,729—an increase of 7060 or 551 per cent. For the Order as a whole the increase for the same period had been at the rate of 230 per cent and he would ask if it were not possible for Lancashire itself to obtain that high percentage. The signs were that the members of the Order were awakening to their possibilities as well as to their responsibilities and in the present year there was no lack of effort in extending the Order. The number of tents instituted during the year was 250 of which ever 100 were adult tents. If all tho dis- tricts in Lancashire had progressed at the same rate as the Warrington district the order would occupy a much stronger position in the county. Fifteen years ago the returns for the Warrington district showed a member- ship of 2e6, while at the end of 1904 the numbers stood at 1237—an increase of about 300 per cent. Warrington had therefore done its duty and deserved credit for that. (Applause). Bro. Churchill dealt at some length with the effects of the Workmen's Compensation Act on the work of the Order, pouting out that unintention- ally the Act led men to be longer in recovery from accidents of a minor character than they would be if they were not entitled to compensation. One of the most disgraceful points of the Act was that a man could not be paid compensation until he had been ill for a fortnight. The Act touched every friendly seciety and the plan adopted in some districts of their Order— which he considered was a fair way of meeting the difficulty—was to give only half the sick pay pro- vided by the scale to a man who was receiving com- pensation under the Act. Continuing Bro. Churchill said he believed that taking the Order as a whole it corn- pared wonderfully favourably with any other society in the interest that the members took in its advancement. He believed the day was not far distant when, if they continued faithful to their covenant, the Order would be a great power for the social well being of the country. (Applause.) An interesting discussion followed, various points of organization being touched upon. It was pointed out by Bros. Massey and Wright, of Warrington, that the Warriogton District had made very gratifying progress but there were still great openings for active work. At the conclusion of the meeting tea was served' In the evening a public meeting and concert took place and there was a good attendance.lA very enjoyable Programme was submitted,songs being rendered by Miss Plinston. Mr. Gough, and Bro. Johnson (Heywood) while Bro. Sutton gave a recitation. The accompanist was Bro. Hill. 1 An address was delivered by the High Chief Ruler who dealt more particularly with the temperance side of the Order's work, expressing the belief that the Order might be made a great and powerful organization for fighting against the liquor traffic. Emphasing the importance of inducing women to join the Order, he said it wise to the great shame of the male population of the land that they had to deplore such an increase of drunkenness amongst women. Con- tinuing, he said they heard very often of the disunion of the temperance party in contrast with the united action of the brewers and publicans, and it was unfortu- nate that such should he the case. The late Sir William Harcourt said that of all the parties and sub-parties that he had to deal with the most untrustworthy was the temperance party. He (the speaker) knew from his own experience of teruperace work that they might very often have men talking hard against the drink but when it came to a question of voting,temperance did not enter into their consideration and they voted merely on party political grounds. Almost in every crisis there seemed to be someone or other on the temperance side who drew some kind of red herring across the path of the temperance reformers. Lately they had had the exhibition of men and women who had been trusted leaders in the fight against the drink evil, seriously proposing Ps a means of combating that evil that we should nmnicipalise the traffic. He said without fear of real contradiction that if an angel from heaven came down upon the earth and sold drink, drunkenness and all its attendant evils would follow. The opportunity would shortly be given his hearers of expressing their views by vote, and he would impress upon them not to listen to any specious arguments to the effect that any elected body which had no direct interest in the quantity of drink sold, was going to regu- late the traffic better than it had been regulated in the past. If we once embarked on the municipal control of the traffic, the authorities would hesitate at closing public-houses, owing to the effect on the rates. The foundation of the Rechabite Order was total abstin- ence on the part of the individual member, and total prohibition for the State. They were net going to get prohibition for a long time yet, but if they would only be more earnest and determined in their efforts, that day would be brought much nearer. They believed in the people having the right to express by voice and vote whether they were going to have the drink in their immediate neighbourhood or not. As the very first instalment of reform they must have given back to the magistrates the power of refusine the renewal of a licence whenever they thought fit. They had heard that the prospective Liberal Government had no programme as far as the temperance party was concerned. The first part of its work was destructive—it had to destroy the ; bad work that had been done by the most talented and the very best Government we ever had. (Laughter.) If the new Government vas going to satisfy the temperance party, it would have to break !down the question of compensation, allowing whatever time limit it liked. But the time must come when no legalisAd compensation should be paid for the non-renewal of licences. Bro. Churchill concluded by urging his hearers to do their utmost in the fight against the brewing interest which was the strongest in the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>BRO. CHURCHILL AT PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>BRO. CHURCHILL AT PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>The High Chief Ruler visited the "Excelsior " Tent No. 2879 Padgate, on Friday evening on the occasion of a tea meeting and entertainment in the Padgato Wes- leyan school. Nearly 100 sat down to an excellent tea provided under the superintendence of Mrs. Ward. The concert was presided over by Bro. John Naylor, of Besston,Cheshire.Au address was given by Bro. Churchill, who spokeof advantages of the society both to juveniles and adults. He also touched upon the Order's very strong financial position, and claimed that the accumu- lated funds per member made it second to none among friendly societies. An interesting programme WAS submitted, the vocalists being Miss Mason (soprano), Miss Gandy (contralto),and Mr.E. Worrall, of Runcorn (tenor). Mr. W. Rate, of Culcheth, gave humorous songs, and Bro. D. Ellam Lancashire dialect sketches. Recit- ations were also contributed by Sisters May Dawson and Maggie Smith. Miss D. Mold, Frodsham, rendered a pianoforte solo, and also acted as accompanist. During the evening Bro. T. VVorral I was presented with a beautiful framed certificate of merit, in recegnition of his services as treasurer since the inauguration of the tent six years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Widnes is usually one of the most attractive away fixtures of the season, but in their engagement at Chemicalopolis on Saturday Warrington had not a very large following to encourage them. Evidently the team's indifferent form of late is result- ing in a decline of interest for even the most ardent enthusiast tires of following a losing team. In this match, however, the Wirepullers easily asserted them- selves and were victorious by eight points to nil. Warrington played a much better game than on the previous Saturday at Swinton. The forwards showed a welcome improvement although their form still leaves much to be desired. A special word is due to Swift who was without,doubt the best forward on the field, tackling and following up with wonderful smartness. Preston and Thomas were also prominent with good work while Hartley signalised his first appearance by incurring the dis- pleasure of the referee and receiving marching orders." .</p>
        <p>A. great deal of unnecessary feeling tended to spoil the match at one stage—in fact for ten minutes in the second half the gant degenerated almost into a fight. Harris managed to come to blows with Lynch and the two were ordered off. Somehow this seemed to put other players out of temper and Argent and Hartley had a little bout which i resulted n their retirement. It is such episodes as these which bring football into disrepute and it is a pity if teams cannot meet in friendly rivalry without resorting to fights if events are not altogether to their liking.</p>
        <p>The issue of the match was hardly ever in doubt, the home team being quite out-classed. As a matter of fact Warrington placed ten points to their credit, Preston kicking a penalty goal which was; however, disallowed as one of the Warringtonians was ruled to be off-side. To deal with individual merit, Brooks and Hockenhall easily over-played their opponents at half back. This is, of course, Brooks's right position and he gave an exceedingly good account of himself. On the three quarter line, Isherwood was in splendid form. He played with all his old dash and was quite the best three quarter on the field. Widdeson was reliable,though not brilliant, and scored one of the tries. The only really dangerous man on the Widnes side was I. Taylor who at centre played a capital all-round game. The League table up-to-date is as follows :--- For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centago Oldham l4 11 2 12321 41 165 12 5 39 82•1.4 Leeds Bro'ton R 11 9 2 01818 43 165 11 11 55 8181 Runcorn Halifax.— 11 7 1 31714 20 88 7 5 29 7727 Bradford ll 7 2 21622 39 161 10 11 53 7272 Hull K.R l2 8 3 11717 16 82 10 15 65 70.83 Leigh Hunslet 9 6 3 01215 20 90 616 GO 66.66 Keighley l2 8 4 01612 23 93 10 10 50 66.66 Salford ll 7 4 01420 32 136 14 S 52 63'63 Swinton.— 12 7 4 11513 11 68 913 57 62.50 Hull St. Helens 9 5 4 01015 23 99 13 15 71 5555 flud'field ll 5 4 21212 28 108 10 14 62 5454 Wigan lO 5 5 01015 23 99 12 18 73 50.00 Warrington 13 6 6 11315 20 90 12 19, 81 50'00 Wak'field Tl 2 4 5 31114 12 64 17 24 106 4583 Bramley Dewsbury l3 5 7 11111 17 73 13 22 92 4230 York l2 4 7 1 912 11 57 10 18 74 37-95 Ctuitleford 8 2 5 1, 5 7 4 26 13 24 98 3125 Millom 6 1 4 1 3 6 7 33 14 21 t 2500 Pontefract 10 2 7 1 5 7 5 29 14 16 76 2500 Morecambe 10 2 7 1 5 715 59 17 33 133 2500 Normanton 9 1 6 2 4 3 1 914 23 97 22.22 Barrow lO 2 8 0 4 610 42 17 33 133 2000. Batley lO 1 8 1 3 7 3 23 19 19 95 15.00 Rochdalell 13 010 3 3 3 9 33 15 38 144 11.53 Widnes 9 1 8 0 2 2 4 16 13 20 86 11'11 Bri,ghousen 10 0 9 1 1 7 63220 39 157 0.00 The points are two for a win and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>Saturday's match at Widnes counted, of course, in the South-west Lancashire Competition. Warrington are rather low down at present, but when it is remem- bered that they have played three of the other five clubs away from home and only two at Warrington it will be seen that they have a fair chance yet of finish- ing on top. To-day should produce another couple of points in the tourney. The South-West Lancashire table up-to-date is as follows : Points. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. Runcorn - 13 .- 6 Wigan - 1 -. 0 -. 52-- 31 ... 6 Leigh 5 3 .. 2 -. 0 -. 36-- 27 -. 6 Warrington % 4.- 2-. 2-. 0-. 20-- 16 _. 4 Widnes 5 ... 1 ... 4 ... 0 11— 40 ... 2 St. Helens</p>
        <p>A very good gam 3 was provided for the Wilders- , pool patrons on Saturday when the "A" team twelve opposed fifteen of the Junior League. The Combination Champions won in the end by 8 points to 5 points and preserved their unbeaten record. 'Jones scored both tries and played a strong game. The Juniors showed an excellent-knowledge of Rugby I and went in, for running and passing on the most approved lines. The threequarters,flutchinson,Oakes, Sankey and Cartwright were all smart, and Clegg dis- , tinguisbed himself at half. Warrington have secured the transfer from Widnes of Charles Turner, threequarter, who is a Warrington lad.</p>
        <p>The return match with Runcorn is down for clecißion at Warrington to-day, and I have little doubt that the homesters will avenge themselves for the narrow defeat at Irwell-lane. Owing to the fracas at Widnes, Harris will be an absentee and Kenyon comes in again. Jones is being given a trial at centre, and high hopes are entertained of him =king a creditable</p>
        <p>show against his native townsmen. The selected War rington team is : Knight, full back; Widdeson, Isherwood, Jones and Kenyon, threequarters ; Brooks and rfockenhall, half backs ; Boardman, Prestag, Shugars, Harmer, Naylor, Swift, Jolley, and HOE, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>The county match on Saturday last on the Stockton Heath enclosure resulted in a win for Cheshire as expected, the final score reading 4-0. The goals were scored by Blatherwick, Tomlinson, Draper and Blencoe, I The Cheshire side were disappointing, the only for— wards to show anything like County form being Draper and Peel. In the halves Blathet wick and Morton were, ' good, but M'Clelland at wing half although defending soundly, did not render his forwards much assistance. Neither of the full-backs were up to County standard" and had the Westmorland attack been equal to even some of our Cheshire quintettes, there might have been some fun, but perhaps not fun from a Cheshire point of view.</p>
        <p>Westmorland are a weak County, and even though Cheshire lid not show up as expected, the score bat for the play a Hughes in goal must have been much higher.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped that the team to do duty against Lancashire will be composed differently, as I am sure Cheshire can put a much better side into the field than was the case on Saturday.</p>
        <p>This week the Stockton Heath club play New Brighton Wanderers I. and it., when the following teams will represent Stockton Heath :—First (at New Brighton) : R. G. Pierpoint goal ;E. Pierpoint, cap- tain ; and L. Fairclough, backs ; Eric Fairclough, H. Kirkconnel, and J. G. Hatton, halves ; S. Baxter, R. Pierpoint. W. H. Banks, W. Richardson, and T. Day, forwards. Referee, W. Carter. Train leaves Central Station 12.50.</p>
        <p>Second (at home on the Criaket Ground) : F. G. Kertland, goal; P. B. Havvtin and B. Silcock, backs ;. B. Guest, E. Jones, and J. Houghton, halves ;H. Owen, A. Hodgkinson, W. Howard, R. Dolan and W. Lionel,. forwards. Bully-od 2.30. Referee, N. W. Bellamy.</p>
        <p>The second team should win, as they will appear for the first time this season with a representative side, but the first team are facing a strong eleven in New Brighton at home, at the same time I think they may be able to make a draw at the least.</p>
        <p>The dub prospects are at present of the rosiest tints, both from a playing and financial point, and if things turn out as they promise to do. the club will have to think seriously of running these three teams nest season.</p>
        <p>Some of Saturday's results, home side first. Northern 3, Bowdon 1, Stoke 3, Timperley 1. hersal 0, South Manchester 0. Latchford 5, North Manchester 1.</p>
        <p>Latchford met North Manchester at Latchford on Saturday last and as anticipated the match resulted in an easy win for the home team. North Manchester are a new team, but in the opening stages they pressed their more experienced opponents, and in fact, scored first. The visitors 'seldom got near their opponents' goal afterwards, and before half time the home team had secured the lead. Four more Foals were added and the result read Latchford 6, North Manchester 1. Owing to the county match the 2nd Eleven fixture was cancelled. This week Latchford play their return match with South Manchester at Manchester. Team : Goal, %V. Unstvorth ; full backs, W. Knight and T. Dixon ; half backs, W. B. Cobb, J. B. Wilkinson, and A. H. Rathbone ; forwards, W. H. Spensley, G.F.Cliffe, D. Knight, J. Callan, and J. R. Conway. Train leavea Central Station at 1.50.</p>
        <p>2nd Eleven v. Prestwich at Latchford : E. Jones, goal : C. Hale and T. Young, full backs; Joseph Albin- son, J. Wilkinson and James Albinson,half backs; W.B. Smith, H. Warburton, R. L. Jones, A. Allander, and W. H. Pickles, forwards. Bully off 2.45 prompt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-09_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>First Tree-Planting Day.</p>
        <p>Beautiful Warrington is a step—a short one it must be admitted—nearer realization. One of the main objects of the movement is to encourage the planting of trees in streets and open spaces and saturday witnessed the east planting ceremony. The site selected was an open space in Hazel-street, oil Marsh Louse-lane, (kindly pre- sented to the Society by the owner, Mr. R. Hazeldine) which it is hoped to convert into an attractive garden. Numerous trees and shrubs were planted and a number of workingmen who live in the neighbourhood have undertaken to tend them and see that, if possible, no damage is done to them. A large num- ber of members of the Society and others assembled, amongst those present being :—The Mayoress and Miss Bolton the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson (chairman of the Executive Committee), and Mrs. Stevenson, Alderman Bennett, Dr. Markel (chair- man of the Tree-planting Committee) and Mrs. Markel, Miss Bessie Dutton' Mr. John Fairhurst (secretary to the Society) and Mrs. Fairhurst. Mr. L. and Miss Greening' Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst, the Misses Birchall, Councillors Hulse and J. Fulton Smith, Mr. Longland (treasurer) and Mrs. Longlar.d, and Messrs. A. Fawcett and Chas. Plinston (Stockton Heath Garden Society), E. Draper, H. Kingdon, Mosse, E. .7. Hall, H- S. Leyland, W. H. J. Jenkins, H. P. Hughes, H. S. Page, F. Taylor, F. G. Taylor, R. Lunt, and Richard Hazelctine.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon STEVENSON delivered a short speech . in which he expressed the indebtedness of the Society to Mr. Hazeldine for presenting the site and to the . workingmen who had promised to keep the garden in , order. He hoped the boys and girls would take a pride in the garden, and do all in their power to prevent possible damage. It was obvious that it was no good to plant a garden if it was going to be spoiled, and they r, hoped the inhabitants would take a pride in it, and ; realize that it would make the neighbourhood more beautiful. (Applause.) The first tree—a chestnut—was then planted by th- Mayoress. Afterwards trees were planted by Mrs Stevenson, Mrs. Markel, Miss Dutton, Mrs. Smethurst Mrs. Longland, Mrs. Fairhurst, Miss Birchall, Miss , Perr,v, Miss Greening, Mrs. James Lawton, Miss Bolton Mr. H. S. Layland, Mr. Jenkins, Mr. Fawcett, Alder--„, man Smethurst, Canon Stevenson, Mr. Lunt, Mr. Hazel- dine, Miss Taylor, Dr. Markel, Mr. Hughes, and Mr. W. Hopkins (who has undertaken a constant supervision of the garden). Several of the inhabitants of the neigh- bourhood, including a contingent of buys and girls, also participated in the ceremony. Alderman BEs-NETT, in proposing a vote of thanks to the ladies and gentlemen who had planted trees,said they could not have had a more auspicious cc tof r the work of the society, and they were very much grad fled to find that, in spite of the somewhat remote quarte of the town in which they had commenced their expert. ment, so many had attended to assist in it. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the boys and girls who had assisted to plant trees would do all they could to prevent damage being done, and if the parents would also take an interest in the movement they need have no fear as to the result. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SfferntllZST seconded, and the motion we heartily approved. • Miss BESSIE DUTTON responded on behalf of the tree planters, and expressed the belief that in the course of • time the garden would become a very pretty spot. She , hoped all the householders of the neighbourhood would „ assist in the Beautiful Warrington movement by dis- playing window boxes and hanging baskets of flowers and making their houses 11.3 attractive as possible. There will be another tree-planting to-day (Saturday) at 2.30 on the vacant piece of land opposite the entrance . to Victoria Park in Knutsford-road. Sympathisers,a with the movement are cordially invited to attend. songs, recitations, &amp;c., were well given, consisting 0,, "Daddy," Daddy," Miss E. Spruce; "The Village Blacksmith," Mr. E. Taylor; recitation, "Trouble in the Amen Corner," Mr. J. Worrall; song, "Little Hero," Mr. E-. Grounds; duet, "Where are you goinz, my pretty maid, Miss May Isherwood and Master A. Whitfield (encored). Selections on the mandoline and piano were given by Miss Lewis and much enjoyed. Mrs. Hughes and Mr. Spruce acted as accompanists.</p>
        <p>BOOTS C kSII CHEMIST'S 1206 DiAurEs.—The useful diaries published by Boots, are greatly in demand by the public, and for 19011 a larger edition than ever i has been issued. The whole of the paper is of English manufacture and the work of printing and binding is done at the head quarters of the firm at Nottingham. The diaries are marvellously cheap and of excellent value. A coupon for £l,OOO insurance, available for 12 months, is enclosed with every copy. Each diary, addition to the general information, contains valuable extracts and legal opinions on subjects that might occur almost every day. The diaries are made in three sizes—foolscap folio, 'foolscap octavo, and paoket, Ana ere only obtainable at the branches of Boots Casts Chemists.</p>
