        <p>tthgunn oetween cmoctb. lye ellen is belleftwl to be more common among men than among women, one writer on the subject holding that superior colour perception on the part of the female has been transmitted and intensified. It may be remarked that savages cf both sexes seem to be more favourably endowed than civilised men in regard to the colour sense. Their fine perception .is manifest in their war- paint, . their crowns of brilliant flowers, and still more brilliant bird's 4. at he rs, their brightly- stained skins, and parti-coliaared dresses, all in marked contrast to tho more civilised dwellers in the temperate zones. Colour blindness is an important question, not as bearing on navigation alone, but upon every kind of employment in which the security of life and property depends upon accuracy in distinguishro signal.. Defective eyesight has been resnonso.l.l fee •,-anv serinits accidents. and rnnty se aistmgm an at least me primary eoilinurs is an indispensable condition for there entrusted with the direction of vessels and those employed in the tratic on railways.</p>
